/* Question bank PA test (English convenience translation), Wft Wealth. Vorm: [vraag, [[antwoord, uitleg] x4], toetsterm CDFD]; het EERSTE antwoord is steeds het juiste.
   Gegenereerd met tools/vragen-js.py. */
(window.PA_VRAGEN = window.PA_VRAGEN || {})["vermogen"] = [
["Mehmet Yilmaz (34) lives with his partner and their two-year-old daughter in a rented home. He earns €3,400 gross per month as an installer, and his partner works part-time. Their joint account holds €4,000; in addition, Mehmet has unexpectedly inherited €25,000. He wants to invest that amount for his daughter's education. What does the adviser normally discuss first?", [["Keeping part of the inheritance as a buffer for unforeseen expenses", "With only €4,000 in savings and a young family, a buffer comes first, so that Mehmet does not have to sell investments at a bad moment."], ["Investing the entire inheritance straight away in an equity fund for the education", "Without a buffer he may have to sell when prices are low if something goes wrong."], ["Taking out an investment insurance policy with death cover for the education", "A choice of product comes only after the buffer, objective and profile have been established."], ["Fixing an investment horizon of at least thirty years for the whole amount", "The education starts in about sixteen years; a thirty-year horizon does not fit his objective."]], "3b.4"],
["A provider sells private individuals rights to the proceeds of teak trees abroad, which the provider plants and manages. The investors share in the expected timber yield. How is such an investment normally classified under the Financial Supervision Act (Wft)?", [["As an investment object", "It concerns an item or right acquired for consideration, with a return in prospect and management largely by someone else."], ["As a financial instrument, just like a listed share", "Rights to teak trees are not securities or derivatives; they therefore fall under the concept of investment object."], ["As a deposit covered by the deposit guarantee scheme", "There is no bank balance involved, so the deposit guarantee scheme does not apply."], ["As non-life insurance against the risk of failed harvests", "The investor buys a right to proceeds, not cover against damage."]], "1b.13"],
["Lotte de Vries (27) has just graduated and works as a policy officer at a municipality. She lives alone in a rented flat and has received €10,000 from her grandmother. She wants to let that amount grow for two years and then use it to furnish a home she is buying. A deposit offers her 5% interest per year, which is credited each time and earns interest itself. What is her balance after two years, excluding costs and tax?", [["€11,025.00", "€10,000 × 1.05 × 1.05 = €11,025; in the second year Lotte also earns interest on the first year's interest."], ["€11,000.00", "This is simple interest; the interest credited in the first year also earns interest in the second year."], ["€10,500.00", "This is the balance after one year, not after two years."], ["€11,050.00", "This is a calculation error; the correct amount is €11,025."]], "2g.7"],
["When is an investment product generally a structured product?", [["When it combines a bond and derivatives", "A structured product is made up of building blocks, such as a bond and an option, with a pay-out formula fixed in advance."], ["When the fund invests in shares and bonds side by side", "A mixed fund is not a structured product for that reason alone."], ["When it is a savings account with a fixed interest rate and term", "A term deposit is not an investment product and has no derivative component."], ["When the issuer has a high rating", "The rating and stock exchange listing do not determine whether a product is structured."]], "1b.14"],
["Which characteristic belongs to a UCITS (undertaking for collective investment in transferable securities, in Dutch: icbe)?", [["It is open-ended and may be offered with a European passport", "A UCITS must in principle redeem units on request, meet diversification requirements and may then be offered throughout the EU."], ["The fund may be offered only to professional investors", "UCITS are intended for, and accessible to, non-professional investors."], ["The fund invests mainly in non-tradable assets, such as private equity and forests", "A UCITS invests in liquid financial instruments; less liquid investments fit an AIF (alternative investment fund) better."], ["The fund issues a fixed number of shares that the fund never repurchases", "That describes a closed-end fund; a UCITS is open-ended."]], "1b.15"],
["A client achieves a nominal return of 3% while inflation is 3%. What does this mean for the purchasing power of his assets?", [["Purchasing power remains approximately the same", "The assets grow as fast as the price level, so the real return is approximately zero."], ["Purchasing power rises by approximately 3%", "That increase is cancelled out entirely by inflation."], ["Purchasing power rises by approximately 6%", "Inflation and return are not added together; inflation reduces purchasing power."], ["Purchasing power falls by approximately 3% per year", "That would apply with a return of 0%; here the return offsets the inflation."]], "1f.2"],
["Daan Bakker (30) works as an IT consultant and earns €4,800 gross per month. He is single, has a buffer of €15,000 and has €400 left over each month. He wants to start building up wealth now to supplement his pension. He wonders whether he should wait until he can invest a large sum in one go. What advantage does investing monthly in an investment fund have for Daan?", [["The risk of a single unfavourable entry point is reduced", "Daan buys in at constantly changing prices, so one bad moment weighs less heavily."], ["Over time it gives him a guaranteed positive return", "Spreading purchases over time reduces entry risk but does not guarantee a return."], ["His monthly contributions then fall outside the box 3 (savings and investments tax) levy", "Wealth built up periodically also counts in box 3 as normal."], ["He no longer needs an investor profile when receiving advice", "Even with periodic contributions, a profile must be established where there is advice or management."]], "1i.6"],
["What does the deposit guarantee scheme (DGS) cover, in broad terms?", [["Bank balances up to €100,000 per account holder per bank if the bank fails", "The DGS protects savers and account holders against the loss of their balance if a bank fails."], ["Price losses on shares up to €100,000 per person", "Price losses are investment risk and are never covered by the DGS."], ["All of the client's assets at a bank, including securities and safe-deposit contents, without a maximum", "Securities are not covered by the DGS, and the DGS has a maximum."], ["Balances up to €20,000 per person at an investment firm", "€20,000 is the maximum of the investor compensation scheme, not of the DGS."]], "1n.4"],
["An adviser holds an authorisation from the Netherlands Authority for the Financial Markets (AFM) as a financial services provider, but no licence as an investment firm. He therefore falls under the so-called National Regime. Which investment service may he in principle provide under that regime?", [["Advice on units in investment funds and the transmission of orders", "Under the National Regime the adviser may advise on units in investment funds and transmit orders to an investment firm or investment institution."], ["Advising on individual shares and bonds of listed companies", "Advice on individual securities in principle requires a licence as an investment firm."], ["Performing portfolio management with a mandate over the client's portfolio", "Portfolio management is an investment service for which a licence as an investment firm is required."], ["Holding clients' funds and securities in his own name and trading with them", "Holding client funds and securities falls outside what the National Regime permits."]], "1b.16"],
["Henk (68) and Ria (66) Jansen are married and live on their AOW (state pension) and a modest pension. After selling their holiday home, their joint savings account at a single bank holds €180,000. They want to use this money over the coming years to help their grandchildren. Ria is worried about what happens if the bank fails. How much of the balance is in principle covered by the deposit guarantee scheme?", [["€180,000, because each person's share of €90,000 is covered", "A joint account is divided equally; Henk's and Ria's shares of €90,000 each fall within the guarantee per person."], ["€100,000, because the guarantee applies per account and not per person", "The guarantee applies per account holder per bank, not per account."], ["€200,000, because each of them is entitled to the full guarantee", "No more is ever compensated than the actual balance of €180,000."], ["€90,000, because only the first account holder is covered", "Both account holders are covered for their share of the joint account."]], "1n.4"],
["Tom (45) and Lisa (43) de Wit jointly run a general partnership (vennootschap onder firma, vof) for their flower shop. They are married under prenuptial agreement, have two children and want to protect their private assets of €90,000. The vof has a loan for fitting out the shop. Tom asks what the position is regarding liability if the vof cannot repay that loan. How are Tom and Lisa liable as partners?", [["Each jointly and severally with their private assets, for the whole amount", "Partners in a vof are jointly and severally liable; a creditor can claim the whole debt from any one of them, including against private assets."], ["Only up to the amount each has contributed to the vof", "A limitation to the contribution does not apply to partners in a vof."], ["Not at all, because only the vof itself is liable for its debts", "A vof is not a legal entity shielding the partners; they remain personally liable."], ["Each only for half, in proportion to their share", "Liability is joint and several and not limited to a proportionate share."]], "1c.1"],
["Sevda Arslan (40) is a self-employed dentist, married with two children. She earns an average of €110,000 per year and pays a monthly premium for an annuity insurance policy to supplement her pension shortfall. She has also insured premium waiver in the event of incapacity for work. She asks what exactly this involves. How does premium waiver in the event of incapacity for work work?", [["The insurer pays the premium in full or in part, and the build-up continues", "In the event of recognised incapacity for work, the insurer pays the premium in proportion to the degree of incapacity, so the annuity continues to build up."], ["On incapacity for work, the insurer immediately pays out the entire annuity as a lump sum", "Premium waiver is about continuing to pay the premium, not about a lump-sum payment."], ["On incapacity for work, the insurer pays a monthly income until AOW (state pension) age", "That is disability insurance; premium waiver does not replace income."], ["Sevda does not have to pay premium in the event of incapacity for work, but the build-up then stops", "With premium waiver the build-up continues, because the insurer pays the premium."]], "1e.3"],
["What normally happens to the price of an existing fixed-coupon bond when the market interest rate rises?", [["The price falls", "New bonds offer a higher interest rate, so the existing bond is worth less and falls in price."], ["The price rises", "A price rise goes with a falling market interest rate."], ["The price stays the same because the coupon is fixed", "It is precisely because of the fixed coupon that the price responds to changes in the market interest rate."], ["The coupon is automatically increased", "With a fixed-rate bond the coupon does not change during its term."]], "1f.7"],
["Which bond is most sensitive to price changes for an equal change in interest rates?", [["A bond with a long remaining term and a low coupon", "The longer the (average) term of the cash flows, the higher the duration and therefore the interest-rate sensitivity."], ["A bond with a short remaining term and a high coupon", "This has a low duration and is not very sensitive to interest rates."], ["A bond that will be redeemed in three months", "With a very short term, the price is almost insensitive to interest rates."], ["A floating-rate bond that is reset every quarter", "An interest rate that is reset often actually limits interest-rate risk."]], "1f.7"],
["A bond with a nominal value of €1,000 and a coupon of 4% is quoted at 80%. What is the current (direct) yield, rounded?", [["5.0%", "The coupon is €40 per year; divided by the market value of €800 this is 5.0%."], ["4.0%", "4% is the coupon rate on the nominal value, not the yield on the market value."], ["3.2%", "This is 80% of 4%; the calculation must divide the coupon by the market value."], ["20.0%", "This is the price difference relative to the nominal value, not the current yield."]], "2g.7"],
["An investor buys a share for €50, receives €2 in dividend and the share stands at €53 at the end of the year. What is the total return over that year, excluding costs and tax?", [["10%", "The total return is the price gain (€3) plus the dividend (€2), which is €5, divided by €50."], ["6%", "This is only the price gain; the dividend belongs to the total return."], ["4%", "This is only the dividend yield; the price gain is missing."], ["9.4%", "This is €5 divided by €53; the return is calculated on the purchase value."]], "2g.7"],
["Which risk can an investor largely eliminate by diversifying across many different shares?", [["The company-specific risk of a single share", "Setbacks at one company have little influence in a broadly diversified portfolio."], ["The market risk of the equity market as a whole, such as in a crisis", "Market risk (systematic risk) affects all shares and cannot be diversified away."], ["The inflation risk across the entire portfolio", "Inflation affects the whole economy and does not disappear by buying more shares."], ["The interest-rate risk of the bond market", "Interest-rate risk is not eliminated by diversifying shares."]], "1j.5"],
["What does the standard deviation of an investment's return express?", [["The dispersion of the return around the average", "A higher standard deviation means larger outliers and therefore more risk."], ["The average return per year", "The average is a different measure; the standard deviation measures the dispersion around it."], ["The total costs of the fund", "Costs are expressed in, for example, ongoing charges, not in the standard deviation."], ["The probability that the issuer of the investment goes bankrupt", "That is credit risk; the standard deviation says something about price fluctuations."]], "1i.1"],
["Which investment category generally suffers most from an unexpectedly sharp rise in inflation?", [["Long-dated bonds with a fixed coupon", "The fixed coupons and redemption lose purchasing power, and a subsequent interest-rate rise depresses the price."], ["Bonds whose coupon and redemption are linked to inflation", "These bonds are designed precisely to limit loss of purchasing power due to inflation."], ["Property with rents that are raised annually in line with inflation", "Indexed rents offer a degree of protection against inflation."], ["Shares in companies that can pass on price increases", "Such companies can partly maintain their profit during inflation."]], "1f.6"],
["What is the difference between an open-end and a closed-end investment fund?", [["Open-end: continuous issue and redemption of units; closed-end: a fixed number of participation rights", "As a result, the price of an open-end fund tracks the net asset value closely, whereas a closed-end fund may trade at a premium or discount."], ["An open-end fund is always sustainable, a closed-end fund never", "Sustainability is unrelated to the legal form of the fund."], ["An open-end fund invests only in bonds and a closed-end fund only in listed shares", "The form says nothing about the investment category."], ["A closed-end fund can issue and redeem unlimited new units every day at net asset value", "It is precisely an open-end fund that does this; a closed-end fund has a fixed number of units."]], "1k.1"],
["Kevin Smit (41) is an account manager, earns €6,000 gross per month and is married with two children. The family has €30,000 in savings and a mortgage on the home. Kevin wants his wealth to grow faster and is considering borrowing €50,000 to invest additionally in shares. He expects the return to be higher than the interest. What is the main drawback of this plan?", [["Losses become larger, while the loan and interest still have to be repaid", "Because of the leverage, a price fall hits Kevin harder, while his debt and interest burden remain."], ["The loan makes his return lower in all cases than without a loan", "If the return is higher than the interest, the return on his own money actually becomes higher; the risk is the problem."], ["For private individuals, investing with borrowed money is not permitted for tax purposes", "That is not so; the loan and the investments simply count in box 3 (savings and investments tax)."], ["He loses the voting rights on his shares to the lender", "The shareholder in principle keeps his voting rights; that is not the core risk of leverage."]], "1g.1"],
["Why can a longer investment horizon generally allow more room for shares in the portfolio?", [["There is more time to absorb temporary price falls", "Over longer periods fluctuations average out more, so the likelihood of a disappointing end result is smaller."], ["Shares cannot fall in value in the long term", "Even in the long term shares can produce a loss; the risk decreases but does not disappear."], ["Lower costs apply with a long horizon", "Costs depend on the product, not on the client's horizon."], ["With a long horizon, an investor profile under MiFID II is not needed", "The profile is always needed; the horizon is actually part of it."]], "1i.6"],
["Gerard Visser (60) is a teacher and earns €5,200 gross per month. He is married and his children have left home. He has €120,000 in an aggressive investment portfolio. In five years' time he wants to work less and use the assets to supplement his income. What is generally an appropriate approach as that moment approaches?", [["Gradually reduce the risk in the portfolio", "With a shorter horizon there is less time to recover from a price fall, so gradually less risk is appropriate."], ["Actually increase the equity holding to achieve more return", "More shares just before withdrawals increases the likelihood that Gerard has to sell after a fall."], ["Invest everything in a single share to limit costs", "That greatly increases the specific risk and does not fit an approaching objective."], ["Leave the allocation unchanged, because the horizon plays no role", "The horizon is actually an important factor in determining the appropriate risk."]], "1i.8"],
["What is meant by rebalancing an investment portfolio?", [["Restoring the agreed allocation across investment categories", "Price movements cause the allocation to drift; rebalancing keeps the risk in line with the profile."], ["Transferring the entire portfolio to another bank or investment firm", "That is a transfer, not rebalancing."], ["Re-determining the costs annually", "Determining costs is unrelated to the allocation of the portfolio."], ["Paying out all dividends received", "Paying out dividends does not by itself change the allocation to the agreed proportions."]], "1p.1"],
["Sandra Mulder (38) is single, works as a nurse and earns €3,900 gross per month. She wants to invest €250 per month for 25 years. Her bank offers an active fund with ongoing charges of 1.5% per year; a comparable index fund costs 0.2% per year. She thinks such a small difference makes little difference. With an equal gross return, what is the effect on her final capital?", [["Through compound interest, the more expensive fund gives a considerably lower final capital", "The cost difference is deducted every year and can then no longer earn a return; over 25 years this adds up considerably."], ["The effect is negligible because the percentages are small", "Small percentages per year have a large influence over 25 years because of the compounding effect."], ["The active fund always yields more, because management pays for itself", "There is no guarantee that active management earns back the higher costs; here the gross return is equal."], ["The costs affect the return only in the first year", "Ongoing charges are charged again every year."]], "1k.5"],
["What is currency risk for a Dutch investor in US shares?", [["The risk that a fall in the dollar reduces the return in euros", "Even if the price in dollars stays the same, a lower dollar yields less in euros."], ["The risk that the US stock exchange closes temporarily and the shares cannot be traded", "That is a form of liquidity or market risk, not currency risk."], ["The risk that the US company goes bankrupt", "That is company risk, unrelated to the exchange rate."], ["The risk that interest rates in the eurozone will rise", "That is interest-rate risk, not currency risk."]], "1f.4"],
["What is liquidity risk for an investment?", [["The risk of not being able to sell quickly, or only with a substantial price loss", "When an investment is thinly traded, the investor cannot always exit at a reasonable price."], ["The risk that the price rises after the sale", "That is missed profit, not liquidity risk."], ["The risk that the investor holds too much cash", "Too much cash mainly causes inflation risk and missed return."], ["The risk that the issuer does not pay interest or redemption on time", "That is credit risk (counterparty risk)."]], "1j.5"],
["What is the difference between an accumulating and a distributing fund?", [["Accumulating reinvests the income; distributing pays it out", "With an accumulating fund the income is reflected in a higher value per unit."], ["An accumulating fund invests only in growth shares", "The term says something about what happens to the income, not about the investment style."], ["A distributing fund guarantees a fixed annual payment of at least 4%", "The payment depends on the income received and is not guaranteed."], ["An accumulating fund falls outside box 3 (savings and investments tax)", "Both types of fund fall in box 3 for a private individual."]], "1l.3"],
["What is generally the risk of a subordinated bond compared with an ordinary (senior) bond of the same issuer?", [["The risk is higher, because in a bankruptcy they are repaid only after ordinary creditors", "That is why subordinated bonds usually offer a higher interest rate as compensation."], ["The risk is lower, because they have priority in a bankruptcy", "Subordinated actually means that they rank behind other creditors."], ["The risk is the same, because the issuer is the same", "The ranking in a bankruptcy makes the risk materially different."], ["There is no risk, because subordinated bonds are covered up to €100,000 by the deposit guarantee scheme", "Bonds are not covered by the deposit guarantee scheme."]], "1g.1"],
["A client wants to know what the correlation between two investments means for diversification. Which statement is correct?", [["The lower the correlation between investments, the greater the diversification benefit", "Investments that do not move in step dampen each other's fluctuations, so the portfolio risk falls."], ["The higher the correlation, the greater the diversification benefit", "With high correlation, investments move in step and combining them yields little risk reduction."], ["Correlation has no influence on a portfolio's risk, only on its return", "Correlation is precisely what determines the effect of diversification."], ["Correlation says something only about the costs of investments", "Correlation concerns the relationship between price movements, not costs."]], "1j.2"],
["What is an essential difference between an investment insurance policy and an investment account at a bank?", [["An investment insurance policy also has an insurance element, such as a death benefit", "As a result, risk premiums and insurance costs are also deducted, which can depress the return."], ["An investment insurance policy has no price risk", "With an investment insurance policy too, the value depends on the investments."], ["An investment account always falls under the deposit guarantee scheme", "Investments are not covered by the DGS, even if they are held at a bank."], ["An investment insurance policy is offered by a bank under a banking licence from De Nederlandsche Bank (DNB)", "An insurance policy is offered by an insurer."]], "1b.10"],
["What was mainly seen as the problem in the discussion about 'usurious policies' (woekerpolissen)?", [["High and unclear costs and risk premiums that significantly depressed the return", "Clients often could not see what part of their contribution went to costs, so the expected build-up disappointed."], ["That investment insurance policies always offered a guaranteed minimum return of 4%", "A guarantee was generally not present; the build-up depended on the stock market."], ["That investment insurance policies did not fall under Dutch law", "The products fell under Dutch law and supervision as normal."], ["That the premium could not be tax-deductible", "The tax treatment was not the core of the woekerpolis affair."]], "1k.3"],
["What is bank saving (banksparen)?", [["Tax-facilitated saving or investing via a blocked bank account", "Since 2008, what could previously be done only with an insurance policy has also been possible via a blocked bank account."], ["Saving in any freely withdrawable savings account, regardless of the purpose of the money", "A freely withdrawable savings account is simply saving in box 3 (savings and investments tax), not bank saving."], ["Borrowing money from a bank in order to invest", "That is investing with a securities loan or leverage."], ["Keeping cash in a rented bank safe-deposit box", "A safe-deposit box is not a blocked tax-facilitated savings account."]], "1b.10"],
["Eline van der Laan (36) is married, earns €4,000 gross per month and invests €20,000 in a US equity fund without currency hedging. She is saving for a larger home in ten years' time. In one year the fund rises by 5% in dollars. In the same year the dollar falls by 5% against the euro. What is approximately her return in euros, excluding costs?", [["Approximately -0.25%", "1.05 × 0.95 = 0.9975; the currency fall almost completely wipes out the price gain in dollars."], ["Approximately +5%", "This ignores the fall in the dollar, which reduces the return in euros."], ["Approximately +10%", "A falling dollar counts negatively, not positively."], ["Approximately -5%", "This ignores the price rise of the fund in dollars."]], "1f.8"],
["Peter and Anja de Boer (both 52) have two children who are students and together earn €110,000 gross per year. In 2010 they bought their home and took out a home-ownership endowment policy (kapitaalverzekering eigen woning, KEW) at the same time. The pay-out is intended to repay part of their interest-only mortgage. Peter wonders whether the tax exemption still applies after the change in the law on 1 January 2013. What is broadly correct?", [["Transitional law applies, so the exemption remains subject to conditions", "For a KEW that already existed before 2013, the exemption is retained as long as the conditions are met."], ["The KEW had to be surrendered compulsorily as of 2013", "There was no obligation to surrender; existing policies could continue."], ["The KEW was automatically converted into an annuity insurance policy as of 2013", "A KEW was not automatically converted into an annuity."], ["Since 2013 they can take out unlimited new KEWs with the exemption", "Since 2013 no new KEWs with the exemption can be taken out, except under the transitional law."]], "1e.2"],
["What does the 1:10 bandwidth requirement for a home-ownership endowment policy (KEW) involve?", [["The highest annual premium is at most ten times the lowest one", "This requirement prevents premium payments from being heavily concentrated in a few years."], ["The pay-out may not exceed ten times the total of the premiums paid", "The bandwidth concerns the ratio between premiums, not the pay-out."], ["The policy must run for at least ten years", "That is not a description of the bandwidth requirement."], ["At most 10% of the premium may go on costs", "The bandwidth says nothing about the costs of the product."]], "1m.4"],
["What is an investment right for an own home (beleggingsrecht eigen woning, BEW)?", [["A blocked investment right for repaying the own-home debt", "The BEW is the investment variant of bank saving for the own home and falls under the same transitional law as the KEW and SEW."], ["A right of the bank to sell the own home", "That is the mortgage lender's right of summary enforcement (parate executie)."], ["A freely withdrawable investment account in box 3 (savings and investments tax) with a higher tax-free allowance", "A BEW is actually blocked and has a special tax status."], ["A right to live in an investment property", "The BEW is a financial product, not a right of residence."]], "1e.2"],
["Which document must a client receive under the PRIIPs Regulation before taking out an investment insurance policy?", [["A key information document (KID)", "An investment insurance policy is an insurance product with an investment component and falls under PRIIPs."], ["An ESIS form (European Standardised Information Sheet)", "The ESIS form belongs to mortgage credit."], ["A Uniform Pension Overview (UPO)", "The UPO is a pension overview from a pension provider."], ["A Standard European Consumer Credit Information form", "That form belongs to consumer credit."]], "1l.4"],
["Which asset categories does the Box 3 Transition Act (Overbruggingswet box 3) use to calculate the notional return?", [["Bank balances, other assets and debts", "Each category has its own notional return, so that the actual proportions are better matched."], ["Savings, own home and pension", "The own home falls in box 1 and pension does not fall in box 3."], ["Shares, bonds and property, each with an equal percentage", "There are three categories, each with its own percentage, and they are classified differently."], ["Box 1, box 2 and box 3", "These are the boxes of income tax, not asset categories within box 3."]], "1m.2"],
["In which box 3 category of the Box 3 Transition Act do shares and a let second home fall?", [["Other assets", "Investments and real estate that is not the own home fall in the category of other assets."], ["Bank balances", "Bank balances are savings and current accounts; shares and property do not fall under them."], ["Debts", "These are assets, not debts."], ["They fall outside box 3", "For a private individual, both shares and a second home fall in box 3."]], "1m.2"],
["What is the main principle of the rebuttal scheme (tegenbewijsregeling) in box 3 after the rulings of the Supreme Court of the Netherlands (Hoge Raad) of June 2024?", [["Whoever shows a lower actual return than the notional return is taxed on the actual return", "This prevents someone from paying tax on a return they have not achieved."], ["If the actual return is higher than the notional return, the taxpayer must always pay the difference", "The scheme works only in the taxpayer's favour where the actual return is lower."], ["The box 3 levy has been abolished entirely for investors", "The levy has not been abolished; only the calculation may turn out differently."], ["Only savers without investments may provide rebuttal evidence", "Rebuttal evidence is open to all taxpayers in box 3, not only to savers."]], "1m.2"],
["Which returns count towards the actual return in the box 3 rebuttal scheme?", [["Interest, dividend and also unrealised changes in value", "The actual return also includes price rises and falls, even if the investment has not been sold."], ["Only realised sale profits", "Unrealised changes in value count as well."], ["Only interest received on savings accounts", "Dividend, rent and changes in value also belong to the actual return."], ["Only the return in excess of the tax-free allowance per person", "The actual return is determined on the entire assets; the tax-free allowance is a different concept."]], "1m.2"],
["Marco Hendriks (55) is a self-employed physiotherapist and earns an average of €75,000 per year. He lives with his wife in an owner-occupied home that they use as their main residence. They also own a holiday home in Zeeland, which they use themselves in the summer. They want to know how both homes count for tax purposes in their wealth planning. In which box do the homes fall?", [["The own home in box 1, the holiday home in box 3", "Only the home that serves as the main residence is an own home in box 1; the holiday home is an asset in box 3."], ["Both homes fall in box 1", "A holiday home is not a main residence and therefore not an own home in box 1."], ["Both homes fall in box 3", "The own home that serves as the main residence falls in box 1."], ["The own home in box 3, the holiday home in box 1", "This is exactly the wrong way round."]], "1m.2"],
["Two fiscal partners have box 3 assets together. What applies in the tax return to the allocation of their box 3 income?", [["They may allocate it in any proportion they wish", "Fiscal partners can allocate their joint box 3 income freely, regardless of who is the legal owner."], ["It is always allocated according to legal ownership", "With fiscal partnership, free allocation is permitted, regardless of ownership."], ["Only the partner with the highest income declares it", "Both partners can declare box 3 income; the allocation is free."], ["It must be allocated exactly equally", "An equal allocation is permitted but not mandatory; partners choose the proportion themselves."]], "2g.2"],
["A central bank cuts interest rates by exactly as much as investors and economists had expected beforehand. What is therefore the most likely reaction of the financial markets to the announcement?", [["A limited reaction, because the expectation was already priced in", "Markets price in expectations beforehand; it is mainly a deviation from the expectation that leads to large price movements."], ["A sharp price rise, because an interest rate cut is always good", "An interest rate cut that was already expected has largely been reflected in prices already."], ["A sharp price fall, because investors do not believe the cut", "There is no reason for a sharp fall if the outcome confirms the expectation."], ["No reaction at all, because interest rates have no influence on prices", "Interest rates do influence prices; here the reaction is limited because the news was already known."]], "1f.9"],
["How do debts count in box 3 under the Box 3 Transition Act, other than the own-home debt?", [["Debts reduce the tax base to the extent they exceed a threshold", "Small debts are thus disregarded; debts above the threshold form a separate category with their own notional return."], ["Debts never count in box 3", "Debts that do not fall in box 1 do count in box 3 above the threshold."], ["Debts are added in full to the assets and so increase the tax base", "Debts reduce the tax base; they are not added to the assets."], ["Only debts to a bank count", "Other debts, such as a loan from family, can also count in box 3."]], "1m.2"],
["What is the tax principle of an annuity (lijfrente) in box 1?", [["The premium is deductible within limits, the payments are taxed", "This is called the reversal rule (omkeerregel): tax is deferred until the pay-out phase."], ["The premium is not deductible and the later payment is therefore untaxed", "That does not fit an annuity for which a premium deduction has been enjoyed."], ["Premium and payment fall in box 3", "A tax-facilitated annuity falls in box 1, not in box 3."], ["The premium is deductible and the payment is untaxed", "That would be a double advantage; the payment is actually taxed."]], "1m.1"],
["What is the function of a prospectus in a public offer of securities?", [["To inform investors about the issuer, the product and the risks", "A prospectus provides the information needed for a well-informed judgement of the investment."], ["To confirm that the Netherlands Authority for the Financial Markets (AFM) considers the investment sound on its merits", "The AFM approves a prospectus for completeness and comprehensibility, not for the quality of the investment."], ["To replace the key information document for non-professional investors", "A prospectus and a KID each have their own function; one does not replace the other."], ["To guarantee the minimum return of the investment in advance", "A prospectus provides information and not a guarantee of return."]], "1g.8"],
["Fatima El Amrani (45) works as a lawyer for a large employer and earns €6,500 gross per month. She is divorced and has a twelve-year-old son. Through her employer she has been building up a full pension throughout her career. She wants to save additionally in a tax-efficient way for her old age through an annuity. What does her pension accrual normally mean for her annual allowance (jaarruimte)?", [["Her annual allowance is limited or nil because of her pension accrual", "In calculating the annual allowance, the pension accrual (factor A) is deducted; with a full pension little or nothing remains."], ["Her annual allowance is actually extra high because of her high income", "A higher income increases the premium base, but the pension accrual is deducted from it."], ["She has the same annual allowance as a self-employed person without a pension", "A self-employed person without pension accrual has no factor A and therefore generally more room."], ["Her pension accrual plays no role at all in the annual allowance", "The pension accrual (factor A) is actually a fixed part of the calculation."]], "1b.1"],
["Johan Peters (50) is married, works as an entrepreneur and has deducted annuity premiums for ten years. His policy now has a value of €60,000. He wants to use that money to pay for a renovation of his home. He is therefore considering surrendering the policy in one go. What happens for tax purposes if he does so?", [["The surrender value is taxed in box 1, normally with revision interest (revisierente) on top", "Because Johan has enjoyed a premium deduction, the surrender value is taxed as income and revision interest is also usually due."], ["The surrender value is untaxed, because it is his own money", "The premiums were deducted, so the payment or surrender value is taxed."], ["The surrender value falls in box 3 at the notional return", "Surrender of an annuity with premium deduction is taxed in box 1, not in box 3."], ["Surrender of an annuity is prohibited by law in all cases", "Surrender is possible but has tax consequences."]], "1o.2"],
["How much is the revision interest on surrender of an annuity for which a premium deduction has been enjoyed, if no exception applies?", [["20% of the surrender value", "The revision interest is 20% and comes on top of the box 1 tax on the surrender value."], ["52% of the surrender value", "This is not a percentage of the revision interest."], ["10% of the surrender value", "The revision interest is higher than 10%."], ["The entire amount of premium deducted", "The revision interest is a percentage of the surrender value, not a repayment of the premium."]], "1o.2"],
["What is an annuity investment right (lijfrentebeleggingsrecht)?", [["A blocked investment right intended for a tax-facilitated annuity", "It is the investment variant of bank saving for annuities, alongside the annuity savings account and the annuity insurance policy."], ["A right to deduct annuity premiums in box 3", "Annuity premiums are deducted in box 1, not in box 3."], ["An investment fund that is compulsory for all employees without a pension scheme with their employer", "It is a voluntary tax product, not a compulsory pension scheme."], ["A loan from the bank to pay annuity premiums", "A loan is not an annuity product."]], "1m.6"],
["Esther Kok (48) is a self-employed graphic designer, earns €60,000 per year and does not build up a pension. She lives with a partner and has no children. Last year was a strong year, but she did not pay any annuity premium then. She now has €20,000 freely available and wants to reduce her pension shortfall. What can she do with the premium allowance she did not use in earlier years?", [["Still use it, subject to conditions, via the carry-forward allowance (reserveringsruimte)", "Unused annual allowance from earlier years can be made up within limits via the carry-forward allowance."], ["Nothing, unused annual allowance lapses at the end of the year", "Unused annual allowance does not lapse immediately but can be made up via the carry-forward allowance."], ["Have the unused allowance paid out by the Tax Administration", "Annual allowance is a deduction facility and not a credit that is paid out."], ["Transfer the unused allowance to her partner", "Annual allowance and carry-forward allowance are personal and not transferable."]], "1m.1"],
["Under the Inheritance Tax Act (Successiewet), who is the taxpayer for gift tax?", [["The recipient of the gift (the acquirer)", "The Inheritance Tax Act taxes the acquisition; the recipient must file the return and pay."], ["Always the donor", "In principle the acquirer is the taxpayer, not the donor."], ["The notary who records the gift", "The notary is not a taxpayer for gift tax."], ["The bank to which the gifted money is transferred", "A bank merely executes the payment."]], "1m.7"],
["What does the rate of gift tax and inheritance tax depend on, among other things?", [["On the relationship to the acquirer and the size of the acquisition", "The law has rate groups by relationship and, within each group, a rising rate."], ["Only on the taxable income of the recipient in the year of acquisition", "The recipient's income plays no role in the rate."], ["Only on the age of the donor or testator", "The donor's age does not determine the rate."], ["On the box 3 category in which the assets fell", "Box 3 categories have no influence on the Inheritance Tax Act."]], "1m.7"],
["Wim Dekker (78) is a widower, has a good pension and well over €400,000 in savings. He wants to help his grandson Thijs (22) with an amount for his studies and first home. He is also considering gifting the amount to his daughter instead. Wim wants to know what the difference is for gift tax. What applies to a gift to a grandchild compared with an equal gift to a child?", [["A grandchild pays a higher rate than a child on the taxed part", "Grandchildren fall in their own rate group with higher percentages than children, and their exemption is also lower."], ["A grandchild never owes gift tax on a gift", "Grandchildren also pay gift tax on the portion above the exemption."], ["A grandchild pays the same rate as another acquirer", "A grandchild falls in a more favourable rate group than another acquirer."], ["A gift to a grandchild may be made only with the parents' consent", "No consent from the parents is needed for a gift to an adult grandchild."]], "1m.7"],
["Ruud Vermeulen (82) is a widower and lives in a care home. He has €300,000 in savings and investments and one daughter, Ingrid. In March he gifts Ingrid €50,000 to repay part of her mortgage. Ruud dies four months later. What is the consequence for the tax charged on the gift?", [["The gift is taxed as an acquisition under inheritance law", "A gift within 180 days before death is treated as an acquisition under inheritance law; gift tax paid is credited."], ["The gift is reversed and the money falls back into the estate", "The gift remains valid under civil law; only the tax treatment changes."], ["Ingrid pays both gift tax and inheritance tax, without any credit", "Gift tax paid is credited against the inheritance tax, so there is no double taxation."], ["The gift is fully exempt because of Ruud's death", "There is no exemption; the gift is actually included in the inheritance tax."]], "1m.7"],
["Erik Brouwer (49) is married to Mirjam and is the father of three children aged 10, 13 and 16. He earns €5,500 gross per month and the family has an owner-occupied home and €60,000 in investments. Erik has not had a will drawn up. He wonders how his estate would be distributed if he were to die suddenly. What does the statutory distribution involve, in broad terms?", [["Mirjam gets all assets, the children a monetary claim", "Under the statutory distribution the spouse receives the assets and the debts; the children receive a claim that is not immediately payable."], ["The children immediately receive half of all the assets", "Under the statutory distribution the assets go to the spouse, not to the children."], ["Mirjam inherits nothing, because there are three children", "The spouse is an heir alongside the children and receives all the assets under the statutory distribution."], ["The estate goes to the State, because there is no will", "The State inherits only if there are no heirs up to and including the sixth degree."]], "1b.3"],
["What is the legitimate portion (legitieme portie)?", [["The minimum share to which a child can still lay claim even after being disinherited", "The legitimate portion is a monetary claim against the heirs equal to half of the share on intestacy."], ["The part of the estate that goes to the State", "The State has no legitimate portion."], ["The partner's exemption from inheritance tax", "A tax exemption is something different from the civil-law right to the legitimate portion."], ["The part of the estate that goes to the notary for the settlement", "The notary is entitled to a fee, not to a portion."]], "1b.3"],
["Within what period after death must an inheritance tax return in principle be filed?", [["Within eight months", "The statutory period is eight months after death; an extension can be requested."], ["Within three months", "Three months is too short; the law says eight months."], ["Within two years", "Two years is much longer than the statutory period."], ["No period applies", "A period of eight months does apply."]], "1m.7"],
["Annemarie Wolters (63) has Dutch nationality, is a widow and has two adult children in the Netherlands. Her assets amount to €800,000. She wants to emigrate to Spain to enjoy her pension there. She has heard that the Netherlands no longer levies inheritance tax after she leaves. Suppose she dies five years after emigrating. What in principle applies to Dutch inheritance tax?", [["She still counts as a resident, because the residence fiction applies for ten years after departure", "A Dutch national who dies within ten years of leaving the Netherlands is deemed, for inheritance tax, to have lived in the Netherlands."], ["The Netherlands levies no inheritance tax once someone has emigrated", "Because of the residence fiction (woonplaatsfictie), the Netherlands can still levy inheritance tax after emigration."], ["The Netherlands levies only if the heirs live abroad", "For inheritance tax the testator's place of residence is decisive, not that of the heirs."], ["The residence fiction applies for only one year after departure", "A period of one year applies only to gifts by non-Dutch nationals; for a Dutch national it is ten years."]], "1m.7"],
["Into which three categories does an investment firm classify its clients under MiFID II?", [["Non-professional, professional and eligible counterparty", "The classification determines the level of protection: the more professional the client, the fewer conduct rules apply."], ["Private, business and institutional client, depending on legal form", "These are not MiFID II categories, although they resemble them."], ["Beginner, advanced and experienced investor", "Knowledge and experience count in the suitability assessment, but they are not client categories."], ["Defensive, neutral and aggressive client", "These are possible risk profiles, not a client classification."]], "1d.1"],
["Hugo Blom (50) is married, earns €5,800 gross per month and has €100,000 invested in a neutral portfolio. He wants to retire in fifteen years. His adviser assumes an expected return of 6% per year and a standard deviation of 10%. Hugo wants to know how far the return can deviate in a bad or good year. Between which limits does the annual return lie with approximately 95% probability under a normal distribution?", [["Between -14% and +26%", "Approximately 95% of outcomes lie within two standard deviations: 6% ± 20%."], ["Between -4% and +16%", "This is one standard deviation, with approximately 68% probability."], ["Between 0% and +12%", "These limits do not follow from the standard deviation of 10%."], ["Between -24% and +36%", "This is three standard deviations, with approximately 99.7% probability."]], "1i.2"],
["Robert van Dijk (57) has sold his business and now owns €2.5 million in investments. He is married, has no debts and actively traded shares and options for years. He finds his bank's questionnaires unnecessary and asks to be treated as a professional client. His adviser has to assess what is possible. What applies to this under MiFID II?", [["That is possible, provided he meets the criteria and has been warned", "A non-professional client can be treated as professional on request after an assessment and a written warning about the loss of protection."], ["That is always possible without further assessment, because Robert wants it himself", "The firm must assess whether he meets the criteria for expertise and experience."], ["That is never possible; a private individual always remains a non-professional client", "MiFID II offers a private individual the possibility, subject to conditions, of being treated as professional."], ["That is possible only with the prior consent of the Netherlands Authority for the Financial Markets (AFM)", "The AFM gives no consent per client; the firm assesses the request itself."]], "1d.1"],
["What information must an investment firm obtain under MiFID II for the suitability assessment in investment advice?", [["Knowledge and experience, financial situation, objectives and risk tolerance, and sustainability preference", "Only with this information can it be assessed whether a recommended investment is suitable for the client."], ["Only knowledge and experience with the type of financial instruments concerned, as in the appropriateness assessment", "That is the appropriateness assessment; advice requires a full suitability assessment."], ["Only the desired return, the term and the size of the contribution", "Objectives are one component, but financial situation and knowledge and experience also belong."], ["Only the client's identity, citizen service number (BSN) and address", "Identification belongs to Wwft customer due diligence, not to the suitability assessment."]], "1d.2"],
["What is the difference between the suitability assessment and the appropriateness assessment under MiFID II?", [["Suitability (for advice and management) is broader; appropriateness (without advice) tests knowledge and experience", "Appropriateness is about whether the client understands the risks of the product, not whether it fits their objectives and financial situation."], ["There is no difference; they are two names for one and the same assessment", "They are two different assessments, each with its own scope."], ["The appropriateness assessment is broader and applies specifically to advice and portfolio management", "For portfolio management, the suitability assessment applies."], ["The suitability assessment applies only to professional clients, the appropriateness assessment to all other clients", "The suitability assessment applies to all clients, with the most extensive application for non-professional clients."]], "1d.5"],
["Under what conditions may an investment firm execute orders on an execution-only basis, without an appropriateness assessment?", [["At the client's initiative, for non-complex instruments, after a warning of no assessment", "Execution only is permitted only if all of these conditions are met."], ["The client has investable assets above a statutory amount and extensive investment experience", "The level of assets is not a condition for execution only."], ["It concerns complex instruments such as turbos and options", "Complex instruments are actually excluded from execution only."], ["The client signs a statement waiving all information", "The client cannot waive the information obligations; it is about initiative, product and warning."]], "1a.9"],
["Jesse Koster (24) works as a warehouse employee and earns €2,600 gross per month. He still lives with his parents and has saved €5,000. He heard about turbos through social media and wants to buy a few himself via his online investment account, without advice. He has never invested before. What must the investment firm do?", [["Assess appropriateness and warn Jesse if the product is not appropriate", "A turbo is complex, so without advice an appropriateness assessment of knowledge and experience is needed, with a warning if the product is not appropriate."], ["Execute the order immediately, because no advice is being given", "For complex products, execution only without an appropriateness assessment is not permitted."], ["Carry out a full suitability assessment, including his sustainability preferences", "The suitability assessment belongs to advice and management, not to an order without advice."], ["Refuse the order, because private individuals may never buy turbos", "Private individuals may buy turbos; the firm must, however, assess appropriateness and warn where necessary."]], "1a.9"],
["When must a non-professional client receive a suitability statement under MiFID II?", [["Before the recommended transaction is executed", "The statement explains why the advice suits the client and must in principle be provided before the transaction."], ["Only if the client asks for it", "Providing it is an obligation, not dependent on a request."], ["Only with the annual cost overview", "The annual overview is about costs after the event, not about the suitability of advice."], ["Exclusively for execution-only transactions in complex products", "With execution only, no advice is given and no suitability is assessed."]], "1m.11"],
["What cost information must an investment firm provide to clients under MiFID II?", [["The expected costs beforehand and the actual costs afterwards, at least annually, with the effect on return", "The client must see what the service and the products cost together and how much of the return that takes away."], ["Only the transaction costs per order, immediately after the order is executed", "Service and product costs must also be stated, not only transaction costs."], ["A cost overview only if the client asks for it or lodges a complaint", "Cost transparency is a fixed obligation, regardless of complaints."], ["Only the fund's ongoing charges beforehand, not the costs of the service or the transactions", "It is precisely the sum of service and product costs that must be made transparent."]], "1k.3"],
["Elly Smit (67) has placed her €250,000 portfolio under management with a portfolio manager. She is a widow, lives on her AOW (state pension) and pension, and wants to use the assets for travel and possibly care later on. Elly is classified as a non-professional client. Owing to a sudden stock market fall, the total value of her portfolio has dropped by 10% compared with the start of the reporting period. What must the portfolio manager do?", [["Inform Elly of the fall by the end of that working day at the latest", "In portfolio management the client must be informed if the value falls by 10% compared with the start of the period, by the end of that working day at the latest."], ["Nothing; the fall is only reported in the periodic statement", "A separate and faster information obligation applies to a fall of 10%."], ["Immediately convert the entire portfolio into cash", "The rules require informing, not selling; that could moreover go beyond the mandate."], ["Notify the Netherlands Authority for the Financial Markets (AFM) of Elly's loss the same day", "The reporting obligation applies towards the client, not towards the AFM."]], "2i.4"],
["What applies in the Netherlands to commission in investment services provided to non-professional clients?", [["In principle a ban on receiving and paying commission from or to third parties", "Since 2014 the Netherlands has had a commission ban for investment services, stricter than the European minimum rules."], ["Commission is permitted without limit, provided the amount is disclosed to the client beforehand", "The Netherlands goes further than transparency and in principle prohibits these payments."], ["Commission is prohibited only for execution only, not for advice or management", "The ban applies to investment services broadly, not only to execution only."], ["No rules on commission apply to investment services", "A commission ban does apply."]], "4b.4"],
["An adviser wants to advise clients on the purchase of individual shares and bonds. Which licence is in principle required for this?", [["A licence as an investment firm from the AFM", "Investment advice on financial instruments is an investment service under MiFID II, for which a licence as an investment firm is required."], ["A licence from De Nederlandsche Bank (DNB) as a bank", "A banking licence is not needed to give investment advice."], ["No licence, as long as the adviser does not hold clients' money", "Investment advice requires a licence even without holding client funds."], ["Only a registration with the Chamber of Commerce", "A Chamber of Commerce registration is not a supervisory licence."]], "1b.13"],
["What does product governance (product development and distribution) involve under MiFID II?", [["Developer and distributor define a target market and focus sales on it", "This is meant to prevent products from ending up with clients for whom they are not intended."], ["The AFM approves every investment product before it is sold", "The AFM does not approve products beforehand; the responsibility lies with the developer and the distributor."], ["Only the developer is responsible for the product, never the distributor", "The distributor also has its own obligations in determining the target market and in distribution."], ["Investment products may be sold only to professional clients", "Product governance regulates target markets but does not exclude non-professional clients."]], "4a.2"],
["When may an investment firm call its advice 'independent' under MiFID II?", [["When it assesses a wide range of providers and retains no payments from third parties", "Independent advice is not limited to own or related products and is free of inducements."], ["When it advises only its own products and is fully transparent about this", "Advice limited to own products is not independent."], ["When the client pays a fixed fee per year, regardless of how wide the range of products assessed is", "A fee alone is not enough; a wide range is also required."], ["When, in addition to the AFM licence, it also has a licence from DNB", "A DNB licence says nothing about the independence of the advice."]], "1h.1"],
["An investment returns +10%, -10% and +10% in three consecutive years. What is the compound return over the three years together, excluding costs?", [["8.9%", "1.10 × 0.90 × 1.10 = 1.089; the compound return is therefore 8.9%."], ["10.0%", "This is the sum of the percentages, without taking compounding into account."], ["11.0%", "This is not the correct result of multiplying the annual returns."], ["3.3%", "This is approximately the arithmetic average per year, not the return over three years."]], "1i.5"],
["What does the summary risk indicator (SRI) in the KID show?", [["A risk class from 1 (low) to 7 (high) based on market and credit risk", "With the SRI, investors can compare the risk of different products at a glance."], ["The guaranteed minimum return over the term", "The SRI is a risk class, not a return guarantee."], ["A score from 1 to 10 for the sustainability of the product, based on the SFDR classification", "Sustainability is not expressed in the SRI."], ["The total costs of the product in euros per year", "Costs are shown in a separate section of the KID."]], "1l.4"],
["For which product does no KID have to be prepared under PRIIPs?", [["A directly purchased listed share", "A share is not a packaged product; PRIIPs applies to packaged investment products and insurance with an investment component."], ["An investment insurance policy", "An investment insurance policy is precisely an insurance product with an investment component under PRIIPs."], ["A structured product with capital protection", "Structured products are packaged products and fall under PRIIPs."], ["A unit in an investment fund", "Investment funds fall under PRIIPs; since 2023 UCITS also provide a KID."]], "1l.4"],
["When must a non-professional investor receive the KID?", [["In good time before he is bound by a contract", "The investor must be able to use the information in his decision, so before the purchase."], ["Within a month after the purchase", "Receipt afterwards is of no use for the decision; the KID must be provided beforehand."], ["Only if he asks for it in writing", "Providing the KID is an obligation, even without a request."], ["Only with the first annual report on the portfolio", "The annual report comes after the purchase; the KID comes before it."]], "1l.4"],
["What is the function of the performance scenarios in a KID?", [["To show what the investor may get back under different market conditions", "The scenarios (such as stress, unfavourable, moderate and favourable) give an impression of the range of possible outcomes."], ["To fix the guaranteed final amount of the product", "Performance scenarios are not a commitment or guarantee."], ["To show the historical return of the adviser and the fund over the past five years", "The KID is about the product, not about the adviser."], ["To calculate the personal tax consequences in box 3 (savings and investments tax)", "The KID contains no personal tax calculation."]], "1l.4"],
["Which type of product is referred to as an Article 9 product under the SFDR?", [["A product with sustainable investments as its objective", "In practice Article 9 products are called 'dark green'."], ["A product that promotes environmental or social characteristics, without a sustainable investment objective", "That is an Article 8 product ('light green')."], ["Any product without any sustainability information", "Even products without a sustainability focus must provide information on sustainability risks under the SFDR."], ["A product that invests exclusively in government bonds", "The type of investment does not determine whether something is an Article 9 product."]], "1l.3"],
["What characterises an Article 8 product under the SFDR?", [["It promotes environmental or social features, if companies have good governance", "An Article 8 product does not need to have sustainable investment as its objective, but must explain those characteristics."], ["It has sustainable investments as its express and sole objective, such as an impact fund", "That is the description of an Article 9 product."], ["It has been certified in advance as a sustainable product by the AFM", "The SFDR is a disclosure regime; the AFM does not certify products."], ["It is required to invest entirely in taxonomy-aligned activities", "An Article 8 product need not invest entirely in taxonomy-aligned activities."]], "1l.3"],
["Since 2 August 2022, an adviser providing investment advice must ask about the client's sustainability preferences. Which forms of preference do the rules distinguish?", [["Minimum proportion taxonomy-aligned, minimum proportion sustainable (SFDR), or taking principal adverse impacts into account", "The client can state one or more of these preferences, or state that they have no preference."], ["Only a preference for or against investments in fossil fuels", "The rules work with three defined categories, not with a single sector question."], ["A preference for funds with a green, yellow or red sustainability label from the AFM or the fund manager", "A colour classification is not a legal category of sustainability preferences."], ["Only the choice between Article 6, Article 8 and Article 9 products", "The preferences are defined differently and are not limited to this product classification."]], "1d.2"],
["In what order does an adviser assess suitability and sustainability preferences according to the ESMA guidelines?", [["First suitability on the ordinary criteria, only then the sustainability preferences", "Sustainability preferences may not weigh more heavily than the other suitability criteria."], ["First the sustainability preferences, and only if there is time left the other suitability criteria", "The other suitability criteria come first and are always mandatory."], ["Only the sustainability preferences, because they replace the other criteria", "Sustainability is a supplement to, and not a replacement of, the suitability assessment."], ["The order is free, as long as the client signs", "ESMA states precisely that the traditional criteria are assessed first."]], "1d.5"],
["A client has a clear sustainability preference, but no product in the adviser's range meets it. What is correct?", [["Recommend nothing as fitting; the client may adjust it, which is recorded", "An adjustment of the preference must be the client's choice and be explained in the suitability statement."], ["The adviser may recommend any product and ignore the preference", "Sustainability preferences are part of the suitability assessment and may not be ignored."], ["The adviser must refer the client to the AFM for a choice of product", "The AFM does not give individual advice."], ["The adviser may present the product as sustainable if it almost meets the preference", "That is misleading and contrary to the requirement of correct, clear and non-misleading information."]], "1d.5"],
["What is meant in the SFDR by 'principal adverse impacts'?", [["The harm investments do to the environment, social conditions and human rights", "It concerns the impact of the investment on the outside world, not the effect of sustainability on the return."], ["The negative influence of climate and sustainability risks on the return of an investment", "That is a sustainability risk; principal adverse impacts are precisely about impact on the outside world."], ["The highest cost items of a sustainable investment fund", "Costs are not sustainability impacts."], ["The largest price falls of sustainable funds in the past year", "Price falls are market risk, not adverse sustainability impacts."]], "4b.5"],
["What is the difference between risk appetite and risk capacity in an investor profile?", [["Appetite is the risk the client wants to take, capacity the loss he can absorb", "Both must be established; the profile may not contain more risk than the client can bear financially."], ["Risk appetite concerns costs, risk capacity concerns return", "Both concepts concern risk, not costs or return."], ["They are two words for exactly the same concept", "The difference between wanting and being able is essential for appropriate advice."], ["Risk capacity is relevant only for professional clients and not for private individuals", "The capacity to bear losses must be established for private individuals as well, and especially for them."]], "1g.4"],
["Bas Willems (44) is married, has two children and earns €4,200 gross per month. He has €40,000 in savings; that is almost all of his assets. In two years' time he wants to use it to renovate the kitchen and bathroom. In the questionnaire he indicates that he is willing to take a lot of risk, because he hopes to see the amount grow quickly. What is appropriate?", [["Advise a defensive profile based on his limited capacity", "When appetite and capacity diverge, the lower capacity is decisive; Bas needs the money in two years."], ["Follow his risk appetite and advise an aggressive profile", "An adviser may not blindly follow the preference if the client cannot bear the risk financially."], ["Advise investing additional borrowed money", "That increases the risk further and does not fit a short horizon."], ["Not establish a profile, because his horizon is only two years", "Even with a short horizon, an investor profile must be established when advice is given."]], "1g.10"],
["Mrs Lucas (61) is a widow and wants to have €150,000 invested to supplement her pension. She is willing to explain her objective. However, she refuses to say anything about her income, her other assets and any debts, because she regards that as private. The adviser otherwise knows only her age and address. What must the adviser do?", [["Not give investment advice, because suitability cannot be assessed", "Without information about her financial situation the adviser cannot assess whether advice is suitable, and therefore may not advise."], ["Advise a standard portfolio with average risk", "A standard portfolio without investigating her situation is not suitable advice."], ["Give the advice and record her refusal in the file", "Recording the refusal does not make advice without sufficient information permissible."], ["Estimate her income and assets himself based on her place of residence", "An estimate without information from the client is not a careful fact-find."]], "1d.5"],
["What caveat applies when using the normal distribution to estimate investment risks?", [["Extreme price falls occur more often than the model predicts", "Returns have fat tails; crises occur more often than the model indicates, so the risk may be underestimated."], ["The model greatly overestimates the likelihood of extreme falls", "The opposite is the case: it usually underestimates that likelihood."], ["The model is usable only for savings accounts", "The model is in fact often used for investments, with the limitations mentioned."], ["The model already takes every possible crisis into account", "A model based on average and dispersion does not fully capture exceptional events."]], "1i.11"],
["Nina Postma (36) is a single mother of an eight-year-old son and works as an office manager for €3,300 gross per month. She has €12,000 in savings. She also has a revolving credit facility with an outstanding debt of €8,000 at a high interest rate. She wants to invest her savings in an equity fund so that she can later pay for her son's education. What is the most obvious suitable advice?", [["First discuss whether repaying the expensive credit is wiser", "The expected return on investing is uncertain, whereas repaying expensive credit gives a certain benefit."], ["Invest all the savings, because shares yield more than the interest", "Shares do not certainly yield more than a high credit interest rate, and Nina would then have no buffer left."], ["Take on extra credit in order to invest even more", "That increases her debt and risk and is not appropriate."], ["Disregard the credit, because it is separate from investing", "Debts belong to her financial position and therefore weigh in suitable advice."]], "3c.3"],
["Thomas Groen (52) is an IT manager, earns €7,000 gross per month, is married and has €200,000 in free assets. His adviser has established a neutral profile, because Thomas wants to stop working in ten years. Thomas nevertheless wants to invest most of it in a few technology funds. He sticks to this, even after an explanation of the risks. What must the adviser do in any case?", [["Point out the risks to Thomas and record the deviation from advice", "The adviser must make the additional risks clear and record that the client is knowingly deviating from the advice."], ["Adjust the advice so that it matches Thomas's choice", "Advice is not suitable merely because the client wants it; the deviation must remain visible."], ["Ignore the choice and record nothing, because Thomas decides himself", "It is precisely a deviation from the advice that must be recorded in the file."], ["Report Thomas to the AFM because of his risky choice", "A client's risky choice is no reason for a report to the AFM."]], "1h.4"],
["Joke Hermans (70) is a widow and lives on her AOW (state pension) and a small pension. She has €90,000 in assets and mainly wants to preserve them, so that she can possibly pay care costs later. Her investor profile is defensive. Her grandson advises her to invest in a fund that invests only in shares from one emerging country. Which recommendation is contrary to suitable advice for Joke?", [["Investing a large part in a fund with shares from one emerging country", "A concentrated, highly volatile investment does not fit a defensive profile and the objective of preserving capital."], ["A diversified portfolio of mainly short-dated bonds", "That fits a defensive profile and the objective of preserving capital."], ["Leaving part of her assets in a savings account", "A savings portion fits her profile and possible care costs well."], ["Discussing and updating her profile with her periodically", "Periodic updating is precisely part of suitable advice."]], "1l.9"],
["Mrs Van Rijn (56) is a State Secretary and wants to have €500,000 managed by a portfolio manager. She is married, has two adult children and wants to use the assets in due course to supplement her pension. According to her, the money comes from an inheritance and the sale of a home. The manager starts customer due diligence. What must the institution in any case do under the Wwft?", [["Carry out enhanced customer due diligence, including the source of wealth and funds", "A State Secretary is a politically exposed person; enhanced due diligence is then mandatory, including into the origin of wealth and funds."], ["Refuse her as a client, because a PEP may not become a client", "A PEP may become a client; stricter requirements do apply."], ["Settle for simplified due diligence because of her public office", "A public office actually increases the risk and calls for enhanced due diligence."], ["Only make and keep a copy of her passport", "Identification alone is insufficient; for a PEP more investigation is needed."]], "1a.7"],
["A Wwft institution reports an unusual transaction by a client. May it inform the client about this?", [["No, the institution may not let the client know that a report has been made", "The Wwft contains a duty of confidentiality to prevent an investigation from being frustrated."], ["Yes, the client must always be informed beforehand about the report to the FIU", "Informing beforehand would undermine the report and any investigation."], ["Yes, provided the client gives written consent", "The client's consent does not lift the duty of confidentiality."], ["Yes, but only after a year", "There is no period after which the client may be informed."]], "1a.7"],
["An institution cannot complete customer due diligence on a new client because the client does not want to explain the origin of his wealth. What applies under the Wwft?", [["The institution may not enter into a business relationship or carry out a transaction", "If customer due diligence does not lead to the intended result, the institution must refrain from providing the service and assess whether a report is needed."], ["The institution may proceed if the client signs a statement about the origin of his money", "A statement does not replace the mandatory customer due diligence."], ["The institution may serve the client only via execution only", "The Wwft makes no exception for execution only."], ["The institution must impose a fine on the client", "An institution does not impose fines; it must refrain from the relationship."]], "1a.7"],
["Under the Wwft, who is the ultimate beneficial owner (UBO) of a client that is a legal entity?", [["The natural person with ultimate ownership or control", "The institution must identify the UBO and take reasonable measures to verify his or her identity."], ["The legal entity itself, as contracting party", "The UBO is always a natural person behind the legal entity."], ["The bank where the legal entity holds its account", "A bank is not a UBO of its clients."], ["The notary who executed the deed of incorporation of the legal entity", "The notary has no ownership or control and is not a UBO."]], "1a.7"],
["What does the investor compensation scheme (BCS) cover?", [["Money or securities that a failed institution cannot return, up to €20,000", "The scheme protects against the institution's inability to pay, not against investment risk."], ["Price losses on private individuals' investments up to €20,000 per person per year", "Price losses are investment risk and are not covered by the BCS."], ["Savings balances at a failed bank up to €100,000 per person", "Savings balances fall under the deposit guarantee scheme."], ["Damage from unsound investment advice up to €20,000 per person", "Damage from advice is a matter of liability or the complaints procedure, not of the BCS."]], "1n.4"],
["What is the difference between a strategic and a tactical asset allocation?", [["Strategic is the long-term allocation; tactical is a temporary deviation from it", "The strategic allocation follows from the profile; tactically a temporary deviation is made based on market expectations."], ["Strategic applies only to shares; tactical only to bonds and cash", "Both concepts concern the allocation across all investment categories."], ["Strategic is adjusted daily; tactical is fixed unchanged for the entire term", "It is the other way round: the strategic allocation is stable, the tactical one moves with the market."], ["There is no difference; they are two names for the same concept from the investor profile", "They are different concepts, each with its own function."]], "1i.18"],
["Why are investors' securities generally protected in the event of the bankruptcy of their bank or investment firm, even above the maximum of the investor compensation scheme?", [["Securities are held segregated and fall outside the bankruptcy estate", "Through this segregation investors can generally get their securities back; the BCS is a safety net if that nevertheless goes wrong."], ["The State guarantees all securities without a maximum", "There is no unlimited State guarantee on securities."], ["Securities are covered by the deposit guarantee scheme up to €100,000", "The DGS covers only cash balances, not securities."], ["The AFM buys back the securities at the last price if the institution goes bankrupt", "The AFM has no role in buying back securities."]], "1n.4"],
["Mila Peeters (29) is single, works as a marketer for €3,800 gross per month and has €20,000 invested in a global equity fund. She wants to know when she will have approximately €40,000, so that she can take a long trip. She assumes an average return of 6% per year, which is reinvested each time. She will not contribute anything more herself. What is the best estimate of the time needed?", [["Approximately 12 years", "With the rule of 72, the doubling time is approximately 72 / 6 = 12 years."], ["Approximately 17 years", "That is too long; through compound interest, assets at 6% double after approximately 12 years."], ["Approximately 6 years", "After 6 years the assets have grown by approximately 42%, not yet doubled."], ["Exactly 16 years and 8 months", "That is 100 / 6 and assumes simple interest without reinvestment."]], "2g.7"],
["Kees Mol (52) is married, earns €4,500 gross per month and invests €50,000 in an equity fund for his pension. In the first year the fund falls by 20%. In the second year the fund rises by 20%. Kees thinks he is now exactly back at his original contribution. What is his result over two years relative to his contribution, excluding costs?", [["A loss of 4%", "€50,000 × 0.8 × 1.2 = €48,000; the 20% rise is calculated on the lower amount of €40,000."], ["No gain and no loss", "A fall and a rise of the same percentage do not cancel each other out."], ["A gain of 4%", "Kees is below his contribution, not above it."], ["A loss of 20%", "After the rise in the second year, the loss is smaller than 20%."]], "1i.5"],
["Cor Smits (58) is a lorry driver, married and earns €3,500 gross per month. He has €100,000 in savings, which he wants to use in seven years to supplement his pension. He does not want to take any risk at all and keeps everything in a savings account. The savings rate has been lower than inflation for years. Which risk is Cor mainly running?", [["His real wealth, and so his purchasing power, is decreasing", "If the interest rate is lower than inflation, Cor can buy less and less with the same money."], ["The nominal balance of his savings account will gradually fall", "The balance in euros does not fall; it is about loss of purchasing power."], ["He loses the protection of the deposit guarantee scheme", "The protection does not depend on the level of the interest rate."], ["He is mainly running price risk on the savings", "A savings account carries no price risk."]], "1f.6"],
["Iris Meijer (35) is married, has a three-year-old daughter and earns €3,600 gross per month as a speech therapist. She wants a substantial supplement to her pension in thirty years and can set aside €300 per month. Out of fear of price fluctuations she chooses savings products exclusively. Her adviser calculates that she will probably not reach her goal with the current savings rate. Which risk is often underestimated in her choice?", [["The risk that the target capital is not reached because of too low a return", "Too low a risk profile can lead to a shortfall over a long horizon; that too is a risk."], ["The risk that the bank loses the savings balance in a bankruptcy", "Savings balances are protected up to the maximum by the deposit guarantee scheme."], ["The risk of an unfavourable entry point in the equity market", "Iris does not invest in shares, so this risk does not play a part in her choice."], ["The currency risk on her savings in euros", "Savings in euros carry no currency risk for Iris."]], "1g.11"],
["What is the purpose of a cash flow overview (liquidity planning) in financial planning?", [["Insight into future income and expenditure, so money is available when needed", "With a cash flow overview the adviser sees when the client needs money and can align the horizon and liquidity of investments accordingly."], ["Determining how much box 3 tax the client owes for the current and the coming tax year", "The box 3 levy is only one item; a cash flow overview is about all money flows over time and not only about tax."], ["Establishing which investment funds have achieved the highest return", "The overview is about the client's money flows, not about comparing historical fund returns."], ["Recording the client's risk profile under the MiFID II rules", "The risk profile is established with the suitability questions; a cash flow overview is useful input for that, but it is not a profile."]], "2d.4"],
["Frank and Marloes Verhoeven (both 46) have three children and together earn €95,000 gross per year. Their home has risen in value to €550,000, while their mortgage is still €250,000. Their savings account holds only €3,000. They consider a buffer unnecessary, because they have plenty of equity in the home anyway. Why is that home equity no substitute for a financial buffer?", [["Home equity can be released only by selling or by borrowing more", "In the event of unexpected expenditure, Frank and Marloes cannot use the home equity immediately."], ["Home equity counts in box 3 (savings and investments tax) and is therefore heavily taxed", "The own home falls in box 1; home equity is not taxed in box 3."], ["Home equity falls under the deposit guarantee scheme and is therefore tied up", "The deposit guarantee scheme concerns bank balances, not the value of a home."], ["The bank may use the home equity for repayment without permission", "The bank cannot simply dispose of the home equity."]], "1b.7"],
["Ahmed (39) and Sara (37) Bouzid have two young children and together earn €85,000 gross per year. They want to save for the children's education, stop working earlier and buy a larger home. With their monthly room of €600 they cannot fully achieve all their goals. Their adviser proposes prioritising the goals. What does that involve?", [["Determining which goals come first if the money is not sufficient for everything", "When resources are limited, Ahmed and Sara have to choose which goals are most important or must be achieved first."], ["Giving all goals an equal monthly contribution", "An equal split takes no account of the importance and timeframe of each goal."], ["Choosing, for each goal, the fund that promises the highest return", "Prioritising is about the ranking of goals, not fund selection."], ["Including only the goals for the next five years", "Long-term goals can also be important and are not simply dropped."]], "1d.4"],
["Ellen Hoekstra (42) is a hairdresser with a sole proprietorship and has no partner. Her private account holds €70,000 and the business account of her sole proprietorship holds €60,000, both at the same bank. She is saving this money for a salon of her own. The bank unexpectedly fails. How much is in principle covered by the deposit guarantee scheme?", [["€100,000, because private and sole-proprietorship balances are counted together", "A sole proprietorship is not a separate legal entity; Ellen's balances are counted together, up to the maximum of €100,000."], ["€130,000, because both accounts are each covered separately", "The guarantee applies per person per bank, and the sole proprietorship is not a separate person."], ["€70,000, because business accounts are not covered by the scheme", "Business balances are also covered by the scheme."], ["€60,000, because only the business account counts as a deposit", "The private balance is also a covered deposit."]], "1n.4"],
["On which types of trading venue can financial instruments be traded under MiFID II?", [["A regulated market, an MTF and an OTF", "MiFID II distinguishes these three trading venues; in addition, trading can take place outside a venue."], ["Exclusively on an exchange recognised by the AFM", "Besides regulated markets, MTFs and OTFs are also trading venues."], ["The DGS, the investor compensation scheme and Kifid (Dutch Financial Services Complaints Institute)", "These are protection and complaints facilities, not trading venues."], ["Only via a bank, outside any exchange", "Trading via a bank can run through various venues; this is not a classification of venues."]], "1j.8"],
["For an investment institution, what is the difference between direct and indirect return?", [["Direct: dividend, interest, rent; indirect: price results and value changes", "Direct return consists of the current income; indirect return of realised and unrealised changes in value."], ["Direct: price results and changes in value; indirect: dividend, interest and rent", "This is exactly the wrong way round."], ["Direct: return before tax; indirect: return after deduction of tax", "The distinction concerns the source of the return, not tax."], ["Direct: return in euros; indirect: return in foreign currency", "The currency does not determine whether a return is direct or indirect."]], "1k.4"],
["A client has a savings account in US dollars at a failed bank. Is that balance covered by the deposit guarantee scheme?", [["Yes, balances in foreign currency are also covered, with payment made in euros", "The DGS covers deposits regardless of currency; the compensation is paid out in euros."], ["No, only balances in euros fall under the scheme", "The scheme is not limited to euros; foreign currencies count too, up to the maximum."], ["No, a dollar account falls under the investor compensation scheme", "A dollar balance remains a bank balance; the investor compensation scheme concerns investment services."], ["Yes, but only if the client also has a euro account at that bank", "There is no condition of a euro account; the foreign-currency balance is covered independently within the maximum."]], "1n.4"],
["Which product is NOT covered by the deposit guarantee scheme?", [["Units in an investment fund, held via the bank", "Securities are not deposits; they are held segregated and, in the event of a shortfall, fall at most under the investor compensation scheme."], ["A term deposit with a fixed term of three years and a fixed interest rate", "A term deposit is a bank balance and therefore falls under the DGS."], ["A freely withdrawable online savings account", "This is an ordinary savings balance at a bank and is thus covered up to the maximum."], ["The positive balance on a current account", "A current account is also a deposit within the meaning of the DGS."]], "1n.4"],
["Ruben Jacobs (45) is director and majority shareholder of a private limited company (bv) with two employees. He is married and has €90,000 privately in a savings account at the same bank as his bv. The bv has €80,000 in its business account, intended for investments. The bank fails. What applies to the bv's balance?", [["The bv itself, as account holder, is covered up to €100,000", "A bv is a legal entity in its own right and therefore has its own guarantee, separate from Ruben's private balance."], ["The bv is not covered, because the scheme protects only private individuals", "Legal entities such as a bv are also covered by the deposit guarantee scheme."], ["The balance counts together with Ruben's private balance", "The bv and Ruben are different account holders, each with their own guarantee."], ["The bv is covered only if it has fewer than ten employees", "A limit based on the number of employees does not exist."]], "1n.4"],
["Gerda Bos (66) has just retired, is single and has €45,000 in savings. She wants to lock €20,000 into a five-year term deposit, because the interest rate is higher there. She keeps the rest freely withdrawable. Her car is old and her roof may need to be replaced in the coming years. What is the main drawback of the deposit compared with a freely withdrawable savings account?", [["The money cannot be withdrawn early, or only for a fine", "With a term deposit the money is tied up; early withdrawal is usually not possible or only against a charge."], ["The deposit is not covered by the deposit guarantee scheme", "A term deposit is a deposit and is covered by the scheme."], ["The bank can lower the interest rate during the term", "With a term deposit the interest rate is usually fixed for the term."], ["The deposit carries price risk, like a bond", "A deposit is not traded and carries no price risk."]], "1n.2"],
["A share is quoted at €40 and the earnings per share are €4. What is the price/earnings ratio?", [["10", "The price/earnings ratio is the price divided by the earnings per share: 40 / 4 = 10."], ["0.1", "This is the earnings divided by the price (the earnings yield), not the price/earnings ratio."], ["36", "This is the difference between price and earnings; the price/earnings ratio is a division, not a subtraction."], ["160", "This is price times earnings per share; for the price/earnings ratio you divide the price by the earnings."]], "1i.19"],
["A share is quoted at €30 and pays €1.50 in dividend per year. What is the dividend yield?", [["5%", "The dividend yield is the dividend divided by the price: 1.50 / 30 = 0.05, so 5%."], ["1.5%", "This reads the dividend amount in euros as a percentage; it must be divided by the price."], ["20%", "This is the price divided by the dividend, so the reverse calculation."], ["3%", "No calculation with these figures yields 3%; the correct yield is 1.50 divided by 30."]], "2g.7"],
["Hans Lambers (64) is retired, married and has assets of €250,000. He wants a stable supplement to his pension. He buys a bond with a coupon of 3% at a price of 95% and wants to hold it until redemption at par. He assumes that his return is then exactly 3%. What is correct about his effective yield?", [["Higher than 3%, because on redemption he also receives a price gain", "Hans receives the coupon and on redemption gets back 100% while he paid 95%; that increases the effective yield."], ["Exactly 3%, because only the coupon determines the return", "The difference between purchase price and redemption also counts towards the return."], ["Lower than 3%, because he bought below par", "Buying below par actually gives a price gain on redemption."], ["Impossible to say, because the market interest rate can still change", "If he holds to redemption, the effective yield is fixed at purchase, provided the issuer pays."]], "1l.8"],
["An issuer receives a lower rating from a credit rating agency. What normally happens to the price of its existing bonds?", [["The price falls, because investors demand more return for the higher credit risk", "A lower rating points to a greater likelihood of default; investors then pay less for the bond, so the yield rises."], ["The price rises, because the coupon is automatically increased with a lower rating", "The coupon of an existing fixed-rate bond is fixed and does not change because of a rating downgrade."], ["The price stays the same, because a rating applies only to new bonds", "A rating concerns the issuer's creditworthiness and also affects existing bonds."], ["The bonds are redeemed immediately at nominal value", "A rating downgrade does not lead to automatic early redemption; the bonds remain tradable as normal."]], "1j.5"],
["What is a convertible bond?", [["A bond that the holder can convert into shares under certain conditions", "The holder has the right to exchange the bond for shares of the issuer at a predetermined ratio."], ["A bond whose coupon is adjusted annually to the market interest rate", "That is a floating-rate note, not a convertible bond."], ["A bond that the issuer may buy back at any time", "That is a callable bond; the conversion right is absent."], ["A bond in a currency other than the euro, exchangeable at the exchange rate", "The currency of a bond does not make it convertible; it concerns conversion into shares."]], "1l.3"],
["What is reinvestment risk in a bond investment?", [["The risk that coupons and redemptions can only be reinvested at a lower interest rate", "If the market interest rate falls, reinvesting cash flows yields less than expected, so the total return turns out lower."], ["The risk that the issuer cannot pay the coupon and redemption", "That is credit risk; reinvestment risk is about the interest rate at which money received is invested again."], ["The risk that the bond cannot be sold quickly without a large price loss", "That is liquidity risk; it has nothing to do with reinvesting cash flows received."], ["The risk that the bond's price falls because the market interest rate rises during the term", "That is interest-rate risk on the price; reinvestment risk arises precisely when interest rates fall."]], "1j.5"],
["What does an investor buy when buying a call option?", [["The right, not the obligation, to buy the underlying asset at the strike price", "The buyer of a call pays a premium for the right to buy; he is not obliged to exercise that right."], ["The obligation to buy the underlying asset at the strike price", "A buyer of an option has no obligation; obligations rest on the writer of the option."], ["The right, but not the obligation, to sell the underlying asset at the strike price", "That right belongs to a put option, not a call option."], ["A share with a guaranteed minimum return in advance", "An option is a derivative and not a share; a return guarantee is not part of it."]], "1l.3"],
["An investor buys a put option on a share. What is his maximum loss?", [["The option premium paid", "The buyer of an option can lose at most the premium, if he lets the option expire worthless."], ["The strike price times the number of underlying shares", "That risk may be borne by the writer of the put, who is obliged to buy the shares; the buyer risks only his premium."], ["Unlimited, because the share price can rise without limit", "If the price rises, the buyer lets the put expire unused; his loss is then limited to the premium."], ["Zero, because a put is insurance against a price fall", "A protective put also costs a premium; it is lost if the price does not fall."]], "1g.1"],
["A client writes (sells) a call option on shares that he does not own, a so-called uncovered call. Why is that risky?", [["In a sharp price rise he must buy at a high price to deliver; the loss is unlimited", "The writer receives only the premium but must buy the shares at a high price on exercise; because prices can rise without limit, the loss is not capped."], ["If there is a sharp price fall, he must take over the shares from the buyer at the high strike price", "That obligation belongs to writing a put option, not to writing a call."], ["He pays a high premium, which he loses entirely if the price falls", "The writer does not pay a premium; he receives it from the buyer."], ["The risk is limited to the premium received, but that premium is taxed", "The premium received is the writer's maximum profit, not his maximum loss."]], "1g.1"],
["What happens to a turbo long when the price of the underlying asset touches the stop-loss level?", [["The turbo expires and the investor receives at most a residual value", "When the stop-loss is touched, the turbo expires; depending on the product a residual value is paid out, which can also be zero."], ["The turbo is converted into the underlying shares at the stop-loss price", "A turbo is not converted into shares; it ends and is at most settled in cash."], ["The issuer automatically reduces the leverage so that the position simply continues", "When the stop-loss is touched the position does not continue; the turbo expires."], ["The investor must pay in the loss above his stake", "A characteristic of a turbo is that the loss is in principle limited to the stake; thanks to the stop-loss no top-up is needed."]], "1l.10"],
["A client places a limit order to buy a share with a limit of €25. What does that involve?", [["The order is executed only at €25 or lower", "With a buy limit the client states the highest price he is willing to pay; execution at a lower price is also possible."], ["The order is executed immediately at the best price of that moment", "That is an at-best order; with a limit order the client determines the maximum price."], ["The order is executed only at €25 or higher", "With a buy order a minimum price would make no sense; the limit is precisely the maximum purchase price."], ["The order is executed as soon as the price drops below €25, at any price", "That resembles a stop order that becomes at-best; with a limit order the limit always applies as the maximum price."]], "1j.8"],
["What is the main risk of an at-best order in a thinly traded share?", [["The order may be executed at a price that deviates considerably from the last price", "With an at-best order the client states no price limit; when trading is thin, the available price may be unfavourable."], ["The order is never executed if the limit is not reached", "An at-best order has no limit; the risk of non-execution belongs to a limit order."], ["With an at-best order the investor always pays double transaction costs to the bank and the exchange", "The order type does not mean transaction costs are doubled; the risk lies in the execution price."], ["The bank may refuse the order if the price rises", "The bank executes an at-best order precisely at the available market price; a price rise is no ground for refusal."]], "1j.8"],
["An investor has placed a stop-loss order on a share he owns. What happens when the price reaches the stop price?", [["The order activates and normally goes to market as an at-best order", "With an ordinary stop-loss order, a sell order is activated when the stop price is reached; the execution price may be lower than the stop price."], ["The share is guaranteed to be sold at exactly the preset stop price", "There is no guarantee on the stop price; in a rapid fall the sale may take place at a lower price."], ["The investor receives a price guarantee and the loss is compensated", "A stop-loss order limits loss by selling, but does not compensate loss."], ["The order automatically lapses, because the price has fallen too far", "A stop-loss order is precisely intended to become active when the stop price is reached."]], "1j.8"],
["A listed company carries out a rights issue (claimemissie). What does this mean for existing shareholders?", [["They get a pre-emptive right to new shares, in proportion to their holding", "In a rights issue the company issues new shares; existing shareholders receive rights to prevent dilution or to sell those rights."], ["They are obliged to sell their shares back to the company at a fixed price", "That describes a buy-out; in a rights issue new shares are offered."], ["They receive free extra shares without new capital coming in", "That is a stock dividend or share split; in a rights issue new capital is raised against payment."], ["They receive the right to exchange their shares for bonds", "A rights issue concerns issuing new shares, not exchanging for bonds."]], "1l.7"],
["A company splits its shares in the ratio 1 for 2. What does this mean for an investor who owns 100 shares at €60?", [["He then has 200 shares at approximately €30, with the same total value", "In a split only the number of shares and the price per share change; the value of the position in principle stays the same."], ["He then has 200 shares at €60 and the value of his position has doubled", "A split creates no value; the price per share halves when the number is doubled."], ["He then has 50 shares at €120", "That is a reverse split, not a 1-for-2 split."], ["He has to pay extra to receive the new shares", "In a share split the investor does not have to pay anything; that is the case in a rights issue."]], "1l.7"],
["What does it mean if a share has a beta of 1.5 relative to the market?", [["On average the share moves more strongly with the market than the market itself", "A beta above 1 means that the share is more sensitive to market movements: with a 10% market movement, on average approximately 15%."], ["The share is guaranteed to yield one and a half times the return of the market as a whole", "Beta is a measure of sensitivity to the market, not a return guarantee; in a falling market the share also falls more strongly."], ["On average the share moves in the opposite direction to the market", "An opposite movement goes with a negative beta, not with a beta of 1.5."], ["The share pays one and a half times as much dividend as the market average", "Beta has nothing to do with dividend; it concerns price sensitivity relative to the market."]], "1j.5"],
["What does the Sharpe ratio of an investment fund express?", [["The return above the risk-free rate per unit of risk (standard deviation)", "The Sharpe ratio shows how much extra return a fund achieves for the risk it takes; the higher, the better the reward for risk."], ["The proportion of the investments that meets sustainability criteria", "Sustainability is shown in other measures and in SFDR information, not in the Sharpe ratio."], ["The fund's total ongoing charges as a percentage of average assets under management", "That is the ongoing charges figure; the Sharpe ratio is about return relative to risk."], ["The degree to which the fund's return deviates from the chosen index", "That is the tracking error; the Sharpe ratio compares return with risk."]], "1i.4"],
["A client is considering an index fund. What is meant by the tracking error of such a fund?", [["How far the fund's return deviates from the index", "An index fund aims to follow the index; because of costs and the way it replicates, the return deviates somewhat, and that deviation is called tracking error."], ["The mistake an investor makes by entering at the wrong moment", "An unfavourable entry point is a timing risk for the investor, not a characteristic of the fund."], ["The difference between the bid and ask price of a fund unit", "That is the bid-ask spread; it is about trading costs, not about following the index."], ["The probability that the index itself has a negative return in a year", "A negative index return is market risk; tracking error measures the difference between fund and index."]], "1l.12"],
["What is the characteristic aim of an actively managed investment fund?", [["To outperform the benchmark through its own selection", "An active manager selects investments and weighs them up in order to beat the benchmark, which usually entails higher costs."], ["To follow the composition of an index as accurately as possible", "That is the aim of passive management, for example in an index fund or ETF."], ["To pay out a return guaranteed in advance to participants every year", "An actively managed fund offers no guarantee; the return depends on the market and the manager's choices."], ["To invest exclusively in the highest-rated government bonds", "Active management says something about the management style, not about a restriction to one type of investment."]], "1l.12"],
["A closed-end investment fund is quoted on the stock exchange at €18, while the net asset value per share is €20. What is correct?", [["The fund is quoted at a discount of 10% to the net asset value", "With a closed-end fund, supply and demand determine the exchange price, which can be below the net asset value: (20 - 18) / 20 = 10%."], ["The fund is obliged to repurchase units at €20", "A closed-end fund does not as a rule repurchase continuously at net asset value; that is why a discount can arise."], ["The fund is quoted at a premium of €2 per share", "A premium means a price above the net asset value; here the price is below it."], ["The price is set daily by the manager at exactly the net asset value", "That applies to open-end funds; for a listed closed-end fund the price arises through trading."]], "1k.1"],
["Why do investors often choose indirect property, such as a listed property fund, instead of buying a property themselves?", [["It gives diversification across many properties with a smaller amount and is more easily traded", "A property fund invests in many properties and can be bought and sold via the exchange, whereas a property of one's own requires a lot of capital and is hard to sell quickly."], ["The price of a property fund barely fluctuates, because the value is fixed in bricks", "Listed property actually fluctuates along with the equity market and interest rates."], ["A property fund falls under the deposit guarantee scheme", "Units in a fund are not deposits and are not covered by the DGS."], ["A property fund guarantees a fixed rental income per year", "Rental income may disappoint through vacancies and rent reductions; there is no guarantee."]], "1l.6"],
["In a unit-linked investment insurance policy, the premium is invested in funds of the client's choice. Who bears the investment risk?", [["The policyholder, because the pay-out depends on the fund value", "With unit-linked policies the value is linked to the fund units; if the return disappoints, the pay-out is lower too."], ["The insurer, because with unit-linked it guarantees a fixed final value", "A fixed final value belongs to a traditional endowment policy with a guarantee; with unit-linked the insurer does not as a rule guarantee it."], ["The adviser, because he advised on the fund", "The adviser must advise carefully, but he does not bear the policy's investment risk."], ["Nobody, because the deposit guarantee scheme compensates losses", "Investment losses are never compensated by the DGS; the DGS concerns bank balances if a bank fails."]], "1b.10"],
["Michel Vos (47) is married, has two children and pays a monthly premium for an investment insurance policy. The policy has a fixed death benefit of €150,000. Because of disappointing markets the policy value is now €60,000. Michel notices that the value barely grows, and his adviser points to the risk premium. Why does that premium depress the return additionally when the investments disappoint?", [["The premium relates to the capital at risk, which grows as the policy value falls", "The capital at risk is the difference between the death benefit and the policy value; a lower policy value makes that difference and the premium larger."], ["The insurer may raise the risk premium from age 65", "Age plays a part, but the extra pressure when investments disappoint comes from the higher capital at risk."], ["The risk premium is doubled as a penalty when investments perform badly", "There is no penalty; the premium rises because the insured risk becomes larger."], ["When investments disappoint, the death cover lapses", "The cover does not lapse; it actually becomes more expensive to insure."]], "1n.5"],
["What is the main characteristic of an investment insurance policy with a capital guarantee?", [["A minimum pay-out is guaranteed, but that guarantee costs return", "A guarantee gives certainty about a minimum, but the insurer charges costs for it or limits the upside return."], ["The insurer guarantees that the funds make a positive return every year", "The guarantee generally concerns a minimum amount on a certain date, not a positive return in every year."], ["The guarantee is free, because the insurer passes the risk on to the DGS", "The DGS does not cover insurance; the guarantee is paid for out of costs or a lower return."], ["The client can surrender the policy at any time without cost at the guaranteed value", "Guarantees usually apply only on the end date; surrender in the interim may be at a lower value and with costs."]], "1l.3"],
["An insurer gets into financial difficulties. What protection do holders of life insurance policies in the Netherlands have, in broad terms?", [["No guarantee scheme like the DGS, but supervision and capital requirements", "There is no separate guarantee fund for life insurance; protection comes mainly from DNB's prudential supervision and the capital requirements under Solvency II."], ["The deposit guarantee scheme compensates the policy value up to a maximum of €100,000", "The DGS applies only to deposits at banks; life insurance policies do not fall under it."], ["The investor compensation scheme compensates the full policy value", "The investor compensation scheme applies to investment services, not to insurance, and moreover has a low maximum."], ["The State guarantees all insured payments by law", "A statutory State guarantee on insurance payments does not exist."]], "1b.10"],
["Dirk Jonker (50) is married, earns €5,000 gross per month and has an own-home savings account (spaarrekening eigen woning, SEW) with a balance of €40,000. The money is intended to repay part of his mortgage. His youngest child has just left home and he dreams of a world trip. He wants to withdraw the blocked balance for that. What is correct?", [["The balance is intended for repayment; withdrawal for something else has tax consequences", "A SEW is blocked for repayment of the own-home debt; if used for something else, the tax facilitation lapses."], ["He can withdraw the balance freely, because it is a savings account", "A SEW is not a freely available savings account; the balance is blocked."], ["He can withdraw it without consequences if he informs the bank beforehand", "A notification to the bank does not prevent the tax consequences."], ["The bank must first convert the balance into an annuity", "A SEW is not converted into an annuity to make withdrawal possible."]], "1o.2"],
["What is an important difference between an annuity savings account (lijfrentespaarrekening) and an annuity investment right (lijfrentebeleggingsrecht)?", [["The savings balance is covered by the DGS, the investment right is not", "An annuity savings account is a bank balance; with an investment right money is invested and the DGS does not apply, although the protection of segregated custody of securities does."], ["Only with the investment right is the premium tax-deductible", "Both forms are tax-facilitated; the deduction depends on the annual allowance or carry-forward allowance, not on saving or investing."], ["Only with the savings account are the payments taxed in box 1", "With both forms the payments are taxed in box 1, because the premium has been deducted."], ["The investment right must be taken out with an insurer", "An annuity investment right is held with a bank or investment institution, not as an insurance policy."]], "1n.1"],
["What was meant by 'activating' clients with an investment insurance policy?", [["Approaching clients to have their policy reviewed and adjusted if necessary", "After the woekerpolis affair, insurers had to approach their clients actively so that they could make a conscious choice about continuing, adjusting or stopping."], ["Obliging clients to convert their policy into a bank savings product", "There was no obligation to convert; clients had to be informed and helped to make their own choice."], ["Automatically surrendering dormant policies and paying the money released to the clients", "Automatic surrender could be disadvantageous; activating means precisely letting the client decide."], ["Raising the premium so that the policy still reaches its target capital", "A premium increase is at most one possible outcome after a conversation; activating is approaching and informing the client."]], "3d.1"],
["Karin Prins (55) is divorced, works as an office manager and has two children who are students. She has an investment insurance policy with death cover for her children and a surrender value of €35,000. She finds the costs high and is considering surrendering the policy and investing the money herself. She has no other life insurance. What must the adviser in any case point out to her?", [["The possible tax consequences and the lapse of the death cover", "On surrender, tax consequences may arise and Karin loses the cover for her children, for which she has no replacement."], ["The fact that the surrender value is equal to the premiums paid", "The surrender value depends on the investments and costs and is often lower than the premiums."], ["The ban on surrendering an investment insurance policy before the end date", "Surrender is normally possible, although there may be costs or tax consequences."], ["The insurer's duty to refund all costs on surrender", "No such general duty exists."]], "1o.1"],
["What is the difference between the investment styles value and growth?", [["Value seeks low valuations; growth seeks above-average earnings growth", "Value investors buy shares that appear cheap relative to earnings or book value; growth investors pay more for expected growth."], ["Value invests only in bonds; growth only in shares of small companies", "Both styles concern the selection of shares, not a choice between bonds and shares."], ["Value follows an index passively; growth is actively managed by a fund manager", "Passive or active management is a different distinction from value or growth."], ["Value always pays dividend; growth never pays dividend", "Dividend policy is not a fixed characteristic that distinguishes the styles."]], "1l.2"],
["Two fiscal partners have assets together in box 3. What applies to the tax-free allowance?", [["Each has their own tax-free allowance, so together double", "The tax-free allowance applies per person; fiscal partners can use it together and therefore have double."], ["Together they have one tax-free allowance, because they are fiscal partners", "The tax-free allowance is not halved for partners; each has one."], ["Only the partner with the highest income is entitled to the tax-free allowance", "The tax-free allowance does not depend on income; every taxpayer is entitled to it."], ["The tax-free allowance applies only to bank balances and not to investments", "The tax-free allowance is deducted from the total return base, not per asset category."]], "1m.2"],
["Paul (43) and Ingrid (41) Huisman are married and have a twelve-year-old son. Grandfather has been giving money in recent years, so that their son's savings account holds a considerable balance. Paul and Ingrid have joint authority over him. They wonder who pays box 3 tax on this wealth. With whom is their son's wealth taxed in box 3?", [["With Paul and Ingrid, because they have authority over their son", "The assets of a minor child are attributed in box 3 to the parent or parents with authority."], ["With the son himself, because the account is in his name", "A minor child does not pay box 3 tax himself; the assets count with the parents."], ["With nobody, because the assets of minors are exempt", "There is no exemption; the assets are taxed with the parents."], ["With grandfather, because he gave the money", "After the gift the money no longer belongs to grandfather."]], "1m.2"],
["Linda Kuipers (50) is married and employed. Together with three friends she has set up an unlisted private limited company (bv), in which she holds 10% of the shares. She also has €80,000 in savings and investments. She wants an overview of all her assets for her financial planning. In which box does her interest in the bv normally fall?", [["Box 2, because it is a substantial interest (aanmerkelijk belang)", "An interest of at least 5% in a company is a substantial interest, taxed in box 2."], ["Box 3, because they are shares", "Shares fall in box 3, unless it is a substantial interest, as here."], ["Box 1, because she is employed", "Her employment is separate from her shareholding in the bv."], ["In no box, because the bv pays corporation tax", "The bv pays corporation tax, but Linda is taxed on her interest in box 2."]], "1m.2"],
["What characterises lifecycle investing?", [["The risk is automatically reduced as the end date approaches", "With lifecycle investing, the equity holding is automatically lowered as the horizon becomes shorter."], ["The risk is actually increased as the end date comes closer", "Lifecycle investing actually reduces the risk to make the final outcome less vulnerable."], ["Investment is made only in companies in healthcare and the life sciences", "Lifecycle refers to the investor's stage of life, not to a sector."], ["The investor chooses a completely new profile himself every year", "The characteristic is precisely that the reduction follows automatically along a predetermined path."]], "1l.12"],
["Rob (59) and Mieke (57) van Leeuwen have a mortgage on their own home and together earn €120,000 gross per year. Their children have left home. They have bought a holiday home on the Veluwe and taken out a loan of €150,000 for it. They expect to be able to deduct the interest just as with their own home. How is the debt for the holiday home treated for tax purposes?", [["As a box 3 debt, because a holiday home is not an own home", "Only a debt for the main residence can be an own-home debt; this debt counts in box 3."], ["As an own-home debt in box 1, with deduction of the interest", "A holiday home is not a main residence, so the interest is not deductible in box 1."], ["The debt does not count at all for tax purposes", "The debt counts in box 3, to the extent that it exceeds the threshold."], ["As a debt in box 2, because it is an investment", "Box 2 concerns substantial interests, not private individuals' property."]], "1m.2"],
["A client lets a home with rent protection to a tenant. Why is that home generally valued lower in box 3 than the WOZ value (official property valuation)?", [["Because the rent protection depresses the value; this is reflected in the vacant-possession ratio (leegwaarderatio)", "A let home is worth less than a home delivered vacant; therefore the WOZ value is multiplied by a vacant-possession ratio."], ["Because let homes with rent protection are fully exempt in box 3", "Let homes are not exempt; they fall in the category of other assets."], ["Because the rental income is already taxed in box 1", "Rental income of a private landlord does not fall in box 1; the lower valuation is due to the rent protection."], ["Because the tenant declares half of the home's value for box 3", "The tenant owns no part of the home and does not declare it; the lower value follows from the vacant-possession ratio."]], "1m.2"],
["Bert (62) and Tineke (60) Hofman are married and have €300,000 in savings. Their son Niels (28) needs a new car for his work as a mechanic. They lend him €20,000 under a written agreement and at a market-rate interest rate. They want to know how this counts in their tax return. How does the receivable count for Bert and Tineke in box 3?", [["As other assets, while Niels declares the loan as a debt in box 3", "A receivable from another person is an other asset; for Niels it is a debt in box 3, because the car is not an own home."], ["As a bank balance, because the money was first in their bank account", "After lending it out it is no longer a bank balance, but a receivable."], ["Not at all, because loans within the family stay outside box 3", "Family loans also count in box 3."], ["As an exempt asset, because Niels uses the car for his work", "There is no exemption for such a loan; use of the car for work does not change that."]], "1m.2"],
["How is an annuity policy for which the premiums have been deducted treated for tax purposes as long as there are no payments yet?", [["The value does not fall in box 3; the later payments are taxed in box 1", "A reversal rule means that the build-up stays untaxed: the premium is deductible, the value is exempt and the payments are taxed."], ["The value falls annually in box 3 as an other asset at the value on 1 January", "A facilitated annuity does not count in box 3; otherwise double taxation would arise alongside the taxed payments."], ["The return within the policy is taxed annually in box 1", "During the build-up the return is not taxed; the levy follows only on the payments."], ["The value falls in box 2, because it is an insurance policy", "Box 2 is for substantial interests; an annuity has nothing to do with that."]], "1m.6"],
["Over how many preceding years can unused annual allowance still be made up through the carry-forward allowance?", [["Over the ten preceding calendar years", "The carry-forward allowance looks back ten years, so that someone who previously had little room to contribute can catch up later."], ["Over the three preceding calendar years", "A period of three years is too short; the law uses a look-back period of ten years."], ["Over all years since the start of working life", "The look-back period is limited; unused allowance from more than ten years ago lapses."], ["Only over the immediately preceding calendar year", "The carry-forward allowance is wider than one year; it covers the ten preceding years."]], "1m.1"],
["Marieke Dijkstra (51) is a self-employed translator, single and does not build up a pension. Last year she made a profit of €55,000. Only in February of this year did she have enough money to pay annuity premium within her annual allowance for last year. She wants to deduct that premium in the tax return for last year. Is that possible?", [["Yes, premium from the first three months may be attributed to the previous year", "Premium paid within three months after the end of the year may, on request, be deducted in the return for that previous year."], ["No, annuity premium is deductible only in the year of payment", "A scheme actually exists to attribute premium from the first months to the previous year."], ["Yes, premium may be attributed to last year until the end of this year", "The period is not a whole year but three months after the end of the year."], ["No, a self-employed person has no right to annuity deduction", "It is precisely self-employed people without pension accrual who often use the annual allowance."]], "1m.1"],
["What is the difference between a flat tax and a progressive tax system?", [["A flat tax has one rate; with progression the rate rises with income", "With a flat tax everyone pays the same percentage; with progression higher incomes pay a higher rate on the excess."], ["A flat tax applies only to companies; a progressive system only to private individuals", "Both concepts say something about the structure of rates, not about who pays tax."], ["With a flat tax the rate actually falls as income rises", "That is a degressive system; a flat tax has a single fixed rate."], ["In a progressive system everyone pays the same fixed amount of tax", "A fixed amount per person is a poll tax, not progression."]], "1m.3"],
["In what form must a tax-facilitated old-age annuity in principle be paid out?", [["In periodic instalments, not as a single lump sum", "The aim is an income provision; therefore the payments must be periodic. A lump sum is in principle surrender, with tax consequences."], ["As a single lump sum at AOW (state pension) age", "A lump-sum payment does not fit an annuity; it is meant precisely as periodic income."], ["At the client's choice, as a loan to his own bv", "Paying out an annuity as a loan to a bv does not meet the statutory requirements."], ["Exclusively as a lifelong payment, never temporary", "Besides lifelong payments, temporary old-age annuities are also permitted under conditions."]], "1m.6"],
["Jan Willemsen (56) was married to Petra and paid premium for fifteen years for an annuity insurance policy, for which he received a premium deduction. He dies suddenly, before the payments have begun. Petra has been designated as beneficiary. She works part-time and wants to maintain her income. What can normally be done to avoid a tax settlement on the policy?", [["Use the value for a survivor's annuity for Petra", "If death occurs in the build-up phase, the value can be converted without settlement into an annuity for the surviving dependant."], ["Pay the value out freely to Petra without any tax levy", "A free pay-out of an annuity with premium deduction is taxed."], ["Put the policy in the bank's name until Petra reaches AOW (state pension) age", "Such a construction does not exist and does not prevent the settlement."], ["Transfer the value to the children's savings account", "That is free spending and actually leads to a settlement."]], "1b.3"],
["By when must an old-age annuity in principle commence?", [["In the year in which he is five years older than the AOW (state pension) age", "The payments of an old-age annuity must commence at the latest in the year in which the AOW age has been exceeded by five years."], ["At the latest on the day on which the client himself reaches AOW (state pension) age", "Commencing at AOW age is permitted but not obligatory; postponement is possible up to five years afterwards."], ["At the latest at age 65, regardless of AOW (state pension) age", "The commencement date is linked to AOW age, not to a fixed age of 65."], ["There is no final commencement date; the client chooses himself", "The law does set a final commencement date, so that the annuity really serves as old-age income."]], "1m.6"],
["What has applied since 1 January 2024 to the increased gift tax exemption for the own home, the so-called jubelton?", [["This exemption has been abolished", "Since 2024 the increased exemption for gifts for the own home no longer exists; the other exemptions still do."], ["It has been increased and now also applies to parents over 40", "The exemption has actually been abolished, not extended."], ["It now applies only to gifts from grandparents", "No variant for grandparents has been introduced; the exemption has been abolished entirely."], ["It applies only if the gift is made through a notary", "A notarial deed has nothing to do with this; the exemption has been abolished."]], "1m.7"],
["Wouter (64) and Marjan (62) de Graaf have €900,000 in investments. They want to give their daughter Eva an amount as a gift in order to limit inheritance tax later, but want to keep the money themselves for the time being for their old age. They therefore opt for a gift on paper (schenking op papier). Eva agrees. What is needed for this acknowledgement of debt to be recognised for tax purposes?", [["A notarial deed and interest of at least 6% actually paid annually", "A gift on paper is recognised if it is recorded by a notary and the parents genuinely pay at least 6% interest annually."], ["A private letter, signed by the parents and Eva", "A private deed is not sufficient for a gift on paper."], ["A notarial deed; interest is not needed within the family", "Without actually paid interest of at least 6%, the debt is not recognised on death."], ["A notification of the gift to Wouter and Marjan's bank", "A notification to the bank plays no role in the tax recognition."]], "1m.7"],
["A client gives a large amount to a public benefit organisation (algemeen nut beogende instelling, ANBI). What applies to the ANBI?", [["It owes no gift tax on the gift received", "An ANBI is exempt from gift and inheritance tax on what it receives, to encourage gifts to the public good."], ["It pays the highest rate, because it is not family", "An exemption actually applies to ANBIs, regardless of the absence of a family relationship."], ["It pays gift tax, but the donor may deduct those amounts", "The ANBI pays no gift tax; for the donor a deduction for gifts may exist in income tax."], ["It must declare the gift in box 3", "An ANBI is not a private taxpayer in box 3; the exemption concerns gift tax."]], "1m.7"],
["What three choices does an heir have when called to an estate?", [["Accept unconditionally, accept beneficially or disclaim", "An heir can accept the inheritance in full, accept it with the benefit of inventory, or reject it."], ["Accept, postpone until after the distribution, or have it gifted", "Postponing or having it gifted are not statutory choices; inheritance law knows unconditional acceptance, beneficial acceptance and disclaiming."], ["Choose the statutory distribution, a legacy or usufruct", "These are concepts from inheritance law, but not choices an heir makes about accepting the estate."], ["Accept, have it auctioned or transfer it to the State", "Auctioning and transferring to the State are not choices of the heir when accepting an inheritance."]], "1b.3"],
["Martin Bakker (35) is married and works as a teacher. His father was an entrepreneur and has died; Martin is his only heir. It is unclear whether the estate contains more debts than assets. Martin does not want to be left paying for those debts out of his own savings of €30,000, but would like to receive any surplus. Which choice fits that best?", [["Accept beneficially", "With beneficial acceptance Martin is in principle not liable for the debts out of his own assets and receives any surplus."], ["Accept unconditionally", "With unconditional acceptance he is also liable for the debts out of his own assets."], ["Disclaim", "By disclaiming he also receives no surplus."], ["Do nothing and wait", "Waiting may result in his conduct being deemed to be unconditional acceptance."]], "1m.7"],
["Sander (38) lives together unmarried with his partner Kim (36) in an owner-occupied home; they have a cohabitation agreement. Sander earns €4,500 gross per month and has taken out a term life insurance policy of €200,000 on his own life. He pays the premium himself and Kim is the beneficiary. They want to know what happens for tax purposes on his death. What in principle applies to inheritance tax?", [["Inheritance tax is due from Kim, because Sander paid the premium", "A pay-out from an insurance policy on which the deceased paid the premium counts for inheritance tax as an acquisition by the beneficiary."], ["The pay-out is always free of inheritance tax, because it is insurance", "An insurance pay-out is not automatically exempt."], ["The pay-out is taxed for Kim as income in box 1", "A pay-out from a term life insurance policy is not income in box 1."], ["Sander owes gift tax on the pay-out", "After death it concerns inheritance tax for the beneficiary, not gift tax."]], "1m.7"],
["Two cohabitants each take out a term life insurance policy on the life of the other, each paying the premium themselves and each being the beneficiary. What is the purpose of this cross construction?", [["To prevent inheritance tax being due on the pay-out", "Because the beneficiary paid the premium himself, there is no acquisition from the deceased and the pay-out falls outside inheritance tax."], ["To make the premiums deductible in box 1", "Premiums for term life insurance are not deductible; the purpose lies in inheritance tax."], ["To ensure the pay-out falls under the deposit guarantee scheme", "Insurance pay-outs do not fall under the DGS; that is not the purpose of this construction."], ["To have the pay-out fall automatically under the statutory distribution", "A cross policy falls precisely outside the estate; the statutory distribution plays no role here."]], "1e.2"],
["Monique Tromp (54) has lived together unmarried with Hans (58) for twelve years. They have no children, no registered partnership and no will. Monique has €150,000 in savings and a brother. She dies unexpectedly. Is Hans her heir by law?", [["No, an unmarried cohabiting partner does not inherit without a will", "Under statutory inheritance law only a spouse or registered partner inherits; without a will, her brother inherits here."], ["Yes, after five years of living together Hans is automatically an heir", "A cohabitant does not automatically become an heir even after a long time."], ["Yes, if they lived together in a rented home", "A rented home does not make someone an heir."], ["Yes, but only for half of the estate", "Without a will Hans is not an heir, not even for part."]], "1b.3"],
["Under matrimonial property law, what is the main rule for marriages entered into from 1 January 2018 without a prenuptial agreement?", [["A limited community: pre-marital assets, inheritances and gifts remain private", "Since 2018 only what is built up during the marriage falls in the community; property from before the marriage and acquisitions by inheritance or gift remain private."], ["A full community of property, in which all assets from before the marriage also fall", "The full community was the main rule until 2018; for newer marriages the limited community applies."], ["Complete separation of property without any community", "Without a prenuptial agreement a community does arise; complete separation requires a prenuptial agreement."], ["A community in which only the own home falls", "The community is not limited to the home; it covers all assets built up during the marriage."]], "1m.8"],
["What is the difference between an heir and a legatee?", [["A legatee has a claim against the heirs and is not liable for debts", "A legatee receives a specific asset or amount through the will and has a claim against the heirs for it; he is not liable for the estate's debts."], ["A legatee inherits automatically by law, an heir only through a will", "It is the other way round: a legacy exists only through a will, whereas an heir is also possible by law."], ["A legatee never pays inheritance tax, an heir always", "A legatee, too, may owe inheritance tax on what he acquires."], ["A legatee is always a family member, an heir can be anyone", "A legacy can be granted to anyone, including a charity or a friend."]], "1b.3"],
["Which acquirers fall in the lowest rate group for gift tax and inheritance tax?", [["Partners and children", "Partners and (step)children fall in the most favourable rate group; grandchildren pay a higher rate on the taxed portion."], ["Brothers, sisters and parents", "Brothers, sisters and parents fall in a higher rate group than partners and children."], ["All persons who have lived together for less than a year", "Short cohabitation does not lead to a favourable rate group; it concerns partners and children."], ["Nephews, nieces and friends", "These are distant relatives or third parties; they fall in the highest rate group."]], "1m.7"],
["What does the obligation of 'best execution' under MiFID II involve?", [["Taking sufficient steps to obtain the best possible result when executing orders", "The firm must consider, among other things, price, costs, speed and likelihood of execution, and must have an order execution policy for this."], ["Always advising the investment product with the highest expected return", "Best execution is about the execution of orders, not about the choice of product in advice."], ["Always executing orders on the trading venues with the lowest transaction costs for the firm", "It is about the best result for the client, not the lowest costs for the firm."], ["Guaranteeing that every order is executed at the lowest price of the day", "A guarantee of the lowest daily price is not possible; the standard requires sufficient steps, not a guaranteed outcome."]], "1j.8"],
["What must an investment firm under MiFID II do with telephone conversations that lead or may lead to transactions?", [["Record and retain these conversations, and inform the client about this beforehand", "MiFID II requires the recording of telephone conversations and electronic communications about orders, so that agreements can be verified afterwards."], ["Record these conversations only at the client's request", "The recording obligation does not apply only on request; it follows from the law."], ["Specifically not record these conversations because of the privacy rules", "The GDPR (AVG) permits recording where it is legally required; MiFID II requires recording here."], ["Make only a short summary if the transaction exceeds €15,000", "There is no amount threshold; the obligation concerns all conversations that may lead to transactions."]], "2g.13"],
["How must an investment firm under MiFID II deal with a conflict of interest that it cannot prevent or manage?", [["Disclose it specifically to the client before the service, as a last resort", "The firm must first prevent or manage conflicts; if that is insufficient, it must disclose the specific conflict before the service is provided."], ["Ignore the conflict if the client is a professional client", "Rules on conflicts of interest also apply to professional clients; ignoring them is not permitted."], ["Settle for a general mention of possible conflicts in the general terms and conditions", "A general mention is insufficient; the disclosure must be specific and comprehensible."], ["Give the client a discount on the costs as compensation", "A discount does not remove the conflict and does not replace the duty to manage or disclose it."]], "4b.4"],
["Which factors determine the level of an AOW (state pension) benefit?", [["The number of insured years before AOW age and the living situation", "The AOW is built up per insured year and its level depends on whether someone is single or living with a partner."], ["The last salary earned and the number of years worked for the employer", "The AOW is not earnings-related and does not depend on an employer."], ["The level of assets in box 3 and the value of the own home", "Assets and home have no influence on the level of the AOW."], ["The commencement date chosen by oneself and the level of the employer pension", "AOW age is set by law and the supplementary pension plays no role."]], "1m.5"],
["Which instrument counts as complex under MiFID II, so that it may not be traded on an execution-only basis without an appropriateness assessment?", [["A turbo on an equity index", "Turbos are derivatives with leverage and a knock-out; that makes them complex, so an appropriateness assessment is needed."], ["A listed share of a Dutch company", "Ordinary listed shares are in principle non-complex."], ["A government bond without special conditions", "An ordinary government bond without embedded derivatives or special structure is non-complex."], ["A unit in a UCITS index fund", "An ordinary UCITS is generally regarded as non-complex."]], "1a.9"],
["An investment firm has agreed a periodic suitability assessment with an advice client. What does that involve?", [["It assesses at the agreed frequency whether the portfolio is still suitable", "Anyone who promises a periodic assessment must also carry it out and report on it, so that the portfolio matches the client's current situation."], ["It asks the client every year for consent to the commission ban", "The commission ban is statutory and requires no consent; periodic suitability concerns the portfolio."], ["It no longer needs to provide a suitability statement for new advice afterwards", "A suitability statement remains necessary for every new piece of advice; periodic assessment comes on top of that."], ["It merely repeats the appropriateness assessment of knowledge and experience", "A suitability assessment is broader than knowledge and experience and also covers objectives and financial situation."]], "2i.3"],
["Why must an investment firm also determine a negative target market for a product?", [["To record which clients the product does not suit", "In addition to the positive target market, it must be determined for whom the product does not fit, so that distribution is not directed at them."], ["To be able to exclude products with a negative return", "The negative target market concerns clients, not the product's return."], ["To exclude clients with negative assets or high debts from investing", "It is not a financial threshold for clients; it concerns the characteristics of clients for whom the product is not appropriate."], ["To identify products that do not fall under MiFID II", "Determining the target market concerns clients, not whether a product falls under MiFID II."]], "4a.2"],
["After receiving the suitability report, a client wants to know why the advice suits him. What must that report contain about this under MiFID II?", [["An explanation of why the advice fits his preferences, objectives and other characteristics", "The suitability statement gives an overview of the advice and explains why it fits the client's situation and objectives."], ["Only an overview of the products bought, the purchase prices and the total costs", "A list of products and costs is insufficient; the justification of suitability is precisely the core."], ["A guarantee that the advice will give a positive return within a year", "A suitability report contains no return guarantee; it substantiates why the advice is appropriate."], ["A statement that the client waives his right to complain", "A client cannot waive complaint rights through the suitability report; that does not belong in it."]], "1m.11"],
["What is an important difference between a bank annuity (bancaire lijfrente) and an annuity insurance policy in the pay-out phase?", [["Only an insurer can offer a lifelong, probability-based payment", "A bank annuity pays out over a fixed period; on death the remainder falls into the estate. Paying out for life is possible only via an insurer."], ["Only with a bank annuity are the payments taxed in box 1", "With both forms the payments are taxed in box 1."], ["Only with an annuity insurance policy is the premium tax-deductible", "Contributions to a bank annuity are also deductible within the limits."], ["A bank annuity must be paid out in a single lump sum", "A bank annuity also pays out in periodic instalments."]], "1n.1"],
["Which warning must appear in a KID for a product that is not simple?", [["That the product is not simple and may be difficult to understand", "This comprehension warning must make the investor alert to the complexity of the product."], ["That the investor may return the product free of charge within fourteen days", "A cooling-off period is not the mandatory warning for complex products in the KID."], ["That the product is covered by the deposit guarantee scheme up to €100,000", "A PRIIP is not a deposit and is not covered by the DGS; such a statement would be misleading."], ["That the investor has passed an appropriateness assessment", "A KID describes the product and says nothing about an assessment of the individual investor."]], "1l.4"],
["What has applied since 1 January 2023 to the product information for UCITS investment funds for non-professional investors?", [["They too must now provide a PRIIPs KID instead of the earlier KIID", "The transitional period for UCITS has ended; since then the PRIIPs KID replaces the key investor information document (KIID)."], ["They are exempt from any pre-contractual information obligation", "UCITS funds must still provide pre-contractual information, now in the form of a KID."], ["They now use only an extensive prospectus of at least a hundred pages", "The prospectus remains, but the short KID is mandatory in addition."], ["They may now be offered only to professional investors", "UCITS are meant precisely for the general public; the change concerns only the information document."]], "1l.4"],
["Which product in principle does NOT fall under the PRIIPs Regulation?", [["A pension product for old age recognised under national law", "Recognised pension products and pension schemes are excluded from PRIIPs; their own information rules apply."], ["An investment insurance policy with death cover", "Insurance-based investment products fall precisely under PRIIPs."], ["A structured product with a capital guarantee and index link", "Structured products are packaged and fall under PRIIPs."], ["A turbo or other leveraged product for private individuals", "Derivatives and leveraged products for non-professional investors fall under PRIIPs."]], "1l.4"],
["What does the section on costs in a KID show, among other things?", [["The effect of the costs on the return over different periods", "The KID shows the total costs and their effect on the return, so that the investor can compare products on costs."], ["The commission the adviser receives from the client", "The KID is about the product; the adviser's remuneration is in other information, such as the cost information under MiFID II."], ["A guarantee that the costs will not rise during the product's term", "The KID presents costs on the basis of assumptions, not a guarantee."], ["Only the one-off entry costs, without ongoing costs", "Ongoing costs also belong in the KID; otherwise the cost picture would be incomplete."]], "1k.5"],
["An adviser uses a product's KID as an advertising brochure and adds promotional text of his own. Why is that not in line with PRIIPs?", [["The KID is neutral pre-contractual information and not marketing material", "The KID must be a stand-alone, neutral and comparable document; promotional additions undermine that."], ["A KID may be provided only by the AFM", "The AFM does not provide KIDs; the document comes from the manufacturer and is given via the seller or adviser."], ["A KID may be provided only after the contract has been concluded", "The KID must be provided precisely before the contract is concluded."], ["Promotional text in a KID is permitted only for investment insurance policies", "For no PRIIP may the KID be used as advertising."]], "1l.4"],
["What is meant under the SFDR by a sustainability risk?", [["An ESG event that can negatively affect the value of an investment", "A sustainability risk is an event in the environmental, social or governance (ESG) area that can impair the value of an investment; it looks from the outside in."], ["The negative consequences of an investment for the climate, the environment and society", "Those are the adverse impacts on sustainability factors; that is the other side of double materiality."], ["The risk that a fund loses its Article 9 status", "A change of classification is not a definition of sustainability risk."], ["The risk that the client states no sustainability preference", "The absence of a preference is not a risk to the value of an investment."]], "1g.1"],
["What does it mean if an investment fund is classified as an Article 6 product under the SFDR?", [["It promotes no sustainability characteristics and has no sustainable objective", "Article 6 products must provide information on sustainability risks, but do not present themselves as sustainable."], ["It invests exclusively in taxonomy-aligned activities", "That fits a product with sustainable objectives better; Article 6 characterises precisely products without a sustainability claim."], ["It is prohibited by the AFM for non-professional investors", "An Article 6 classification is not a prohibition; it concerns the degree of sustainability information."], ["It must invest at least 50% in sustainable investments", "Article 6 sets no minimum proportion of sustainable investments."]], "1l.3"],
["Which statement about the EU taxonomy is correct?", [["A system classifying which economic activities are environmentally sustainable", "The Taxonomy Regulation sets criteria per activity, linked to environmental objectives such as climate change mitigation and climate change adaptation."], ["It determines which investors must have a sustainability preference", "The taxonomy concerns activities, not investors' preferences."], ["It gives all investment funds a grade from 1 to 10", "The taxonomy has no grades for funds; it classifies economic activities."], ["It applies only to social objectives, such as working conditions and human rights", "The current taxonomy focuses on environmental objectives, not on social ones."]], "1l.3"],
["Does a financial adviser who gives investment advice or advice on insurance-based investment products fall under the SFDR?", [["Yes, he must state, among other things, how he takes sustainability risks into account", "The SFDR also applies to financial advisers; they must state, among other things, on their website how sustainability risks are included in their advice."], ["No, the SFDR applies only to fund managers", "Fund managers fall under it, but so do insurers, pension providers and financial advisers."], ["No, only if the adviser has more than 500 employees", "The threshold of 500 employees plays a part in the mandatory statement on adverse impacts, not in the question of whether the SFDR applies."], ["Yes, but only if he advises exclusively Article 9 products to his clients", "The SFDR applies regardless of which products the adviser advises on."]], "4b.5"],
["What is meant by 'greenwashing' in the context of sustainability?", [["Presenting a product or company as more sustainable than it really is", "Greenwashing is misleading about sustainability; the SFDR and the rules on misleading information are intended to counter it."], ["Investing in green bonds with a government guarantee", "Green bonds are a product form, not a form of misleading."], ["Excluding fossil-fuel companies from an investment fund", "Exclusion is a legitimate sustainability strategy, not misleading."], ["The mandatory inclusion of sustainability questions in the client profile", "That is a statutory obligation and not greenwashing."]], "4a.3"],
["What is meant by excess-interest profit sharing (overrentewinstdeling) in a traditional life insurance policy?", [["The policyholder shares in the return that the insurer achieves above the technical interest rate", "If the insurer achieves more return than the guaranteed technical interest rate, part of it can be added to the policy."], ["The insurer shares in the policyholder's profit on his savings account", "It concerns the insurer's return, not the client's savings account."], ["The interest on the policy is reduced every year if the insurer makes a profit", "Excess-interest profit sharing can actually increase the pay-out."], ["The policyholder pays extra premium if the market interest rate rises above the technical interest rate", "No extra premium is due if the market interest rate is higher."]], "1n.5"],
["Yvonne Schouten (49) is divorced, works as a project manager and has €70,000 in free assets. She wants to use the money in fifteen years to supplement her pension. In the online questionnaire she states that a fall of 30% would be no problem for her. In the conversation, however, she says that she loses sleep over any loss. What must the adviser do?", [["Discuss and resolve the contradiction before setting the profile", "Contradictory answers must be clarified, so that the profile really fits Yvonne."], ["Rely on the questionnaire, because it was completed in writing", "A written answer does not take precedence if the conversation shows something different."], ["Take the average of the two answers and choose a neutral profile", "Averaging does not resolve the contradiction and may lead to an incorrect profile."], ["Refer her to execution only, because advice is no longer possible", "Advice remains possible after a good conversation about her risk attitude."]], "1g.3"],
["What is the main difference between investment advice and portfolio management?", [["With advice the client decides, with management the manager does, within the mandate", "An advice client takes the decision himself after a recommendation; in portfolio management the manager takes the decisions within the agreed investment guidelines."], ["With portfolio management no suitability assessment is needed, with advice it is", "With portfolio management too, the firm must assess whether the management is suitable for the client."], ["With advice the adviser bears the investment risk, with management the client himself", "In both cases the client bears the investment risk; the difference lies in who takes the decisions."], ["Portfolio management is permitted only for professional clients", "Portfolio management is also possible for non-professional clients, with the protection that goes with it."]], "1h.1"],
["Leo Dam (62) is married, works as a manager and has an advisory portfolio of €180,000 with an aggressive investor profile. When that profile was established, his objective was still twenty years away. At the annual review he says that he will retire in five years. He then wants to use the assets to supplement his income. What does the adviser's duty of care now require?", [["Discussing whether the profile and allocation still fit the shorter horizon", "A shorter horizon is a material change that gives reason to reassess the profile and allocation."], ["Leaving the profile unchanged, because Leo chose it himself earlier", "A profile is a snapshot and must be revised when circumstances change."], ["Immediately converting the assets entirely into savings without consultation", "An adjustment is made in consultation with the client and need not be complete."], ["Reviewing only the portfolio's costs", "The review also concerns objectives, horizon and risk, not only costs."]], "1p.5"],
["Tamara Kroon (33) is a hairdresser, earns €2,400 gross per month and has €10,000 in savings. She has never invested. A friend told her about a structured product with a high coupon that depends on three equity indices. Even after explanation Tamara does not understand how the return and the risk work, but she insists on the product anyway. What is in line with the duty of care?", [["Not advising it, because Tamara does not understand how it works and its risks", "A product that the client does not understand even after explanation is not suitable to advise on."], ["Advising it if she declares in writing that she accepts the risks", "A statement by the client does not make an unsuitable product suitable."], ["Advising it, because Tamara makes the choice herself", "With advice, the adviser must himself assess whether the product is suitable."], ["Advising it if the amount invested is less than €5,000", "The size of the amount invested does not make a product that is not understood suitable."]], "1h.2"],
["A subdistrict court judge (kantonrechter) has placed a client's assets under protective administration (beschermingsbewind). What does this mean for advice on investing those assets?", [["The client can no longer dispose of those assets independently", "Under administration the administrator manages the assets, and the client cannot dispose of them independently."], ["Nothing, the client remains completely free to decide for himself", "It is precisely the disposal of the assets under administration that is restricted."], ["The client may only invest via execution only, without advice", "Administration concerns who decides, not the form of service."], ["The AFM decides from now on on all of the client's investments", "The AFM has no role in protective administration."]], "1a.8"],
["An investment firm wants to keep the client information of its advice clients up to date. What is in line with the rules?", [["Checking periodically that the information is still correct, and earlier on signs of change", "Advice must be based on current and reliable information; it is therefore updated with appropriate regularity and when circumstances change."], ["Recording the information only at the start of the relationship", "One-off recording is insufficient, because a client's situation changes over time."], ["Having the client complete an entirely new profile every quarter, regardless of the situation", "Having everything completed again too often and without reason is not necessary; it is about appropriate regularity and responding to signals."], ["Updating only the client's age annually", "Age is only one factor; objectives, financial situation and risk attitude may also change."]], "1p.4"],
["For how long must a Wwft institution in principle retain the data from customer due diligence?", [["Five years after the end of the relationship or after the transaction", "The Wwft prescribes a retention period of five years, counted from the end of the relationship or the execution of the transaction."], ["Seven years after the start of the business relationship", "Seven years is the tax retention obligation and starts from a different moment; the Wwft has five years after the end."], ["One year after the last transaction", "One year is too short to enable investigations by supervisors and investigative authorities."], ["For as long as the client lives, even after the business relationship has ended", "The Wwft does not link the period to the client's life, but to the end of the relationship or transaction."]], "1a.7"],
["An institution in good faith reports an unusual transaction to FIU-Nederland. The client later holds it liable for damage. What applies?", [["It is in principle protected against liability for a report made in good faith", "The Wwft protects reporters, so that institutions are not discouraged from reporting unusual transactions."], ["The institution must compensate the damage, because it has breached professional secrecy", "A legally required report made in good faith does not breach professional secrecy and does not lead to liability."], ["The institution is protected only if the client is later convicted", "The protection does not depend on a later conviction, but on good faith at the time of the report."], ["The institution is protected only if it informed the client beforehand", "Informing the client beforehand is precisely prohibited; the protection applies without the client having been warned."]], "1a.7"],
["When may a Wwft institution settle for simplified customer due diligence?", [["If the relationship or transaction demonstrably poses a low money-laundering risk", "The Wwft's risk-based system permits lighter due diligence if the risk is low; that assessment must be substantiated."], ["Always when the client has been a regular client of the institution for a long time", "The length of the relationship says nothing about the risk; a regular client may actually present a high risk."], ["When the client is a politically exposed person", "For a PEP, enhanced customer due diligence is mandatory."], ["When the transaction takes place in cash", "Cash transactions generally increase the money-laundering risk; that does not justify simplified due diligence."]], "1a.7"],
["A client of an investment firm announces a large deposit, but withdraws it when asked about the origin of the money. Must the institution assess this as possibly unusual?", [["Yes, an intended transaction can also be reportable", "The reporting obligation also applies to intended unusual transactions; backing out after questions about origin can be a signal."], ["No, only transactions actually carried out can be reported", "The Wwft does not limit the reporting obligation to executed transactions; intended transactions are covered too."], ["No, because the client himself decided not to deposit", "The client's decision does not remove a possible money-laundering signal."], ["Yes, but only after the client's consent", "The client's consent is not needed and asking for it would precisely warn the client."]], "4b.1"],
["Which supervisor in the Netherlands supervises compliance with the Wwft by investment firms?", [["The AFM", "The AFM supervises compliance with the Wwft by, among others, investment firms, managers of investment institutions and financial service providers under its supervision."], ["FIU-Nederland", "FIU-Nederland receives and analyses reports, but does not supervise compliance with the Wwft."], ["The Tax Administration", "The Tax Administration (Bureau Toezicht Wwft) supervises, among others, estate agents and dealers, not investment firms."], ["Kifid", "Kifid handles complaints from consumers and is not a Wwft supervisor."]], "1a.7"],
["Sanne (29) works as a nurse and has €15,000 in savings. She wants to invest €3,000 in crypto via a European trading platform she found online. She asks her adviser what she should check and whether her money is protected if things go wrong. She thinks crypto is protected just as well as a securities account at a bank. What is correct?", [["Whether the platform has a MiCA licence; it does not protect against price losses", "Since 30 December 2024, providers of crypto services in the EU need a MiCA licence; there is no investor compensation scheme for crypto. The licence is supervision of the provider, not a guarantee of value or compensation on bankruptcy as with investments under the investor compensation scheme."], ["Crypto at a licensed platform is covered by the deposit guarantee scheme up to €100,000", "The deposit guarantee scheme applies to balances at banks, not to crypto."], ["No licence is needed for crypto, so Sanne does not need to check anything", "Under MiCA a licence is precisely mandatory for crypto services in the EU; checking whether one exists is a first step."], ["With a MiCA licence the platform guarantees that Sanne gets her contribution back", "A licence concerns the requirements for the platform, not the return; Sanne can lose her contribution in whole or in part."]], "1n.4"],
["Ruben (52) has held crypto at a trading platform in another EU country for a few years. He has never put it in his income tax return, because he thinks the Dutch Tax Administration cannot see it anyway. He asks his adviser whether that is right. What is correct?", [["Crypto belongs in box 3, and since 2026 EU platforms pass client data to tax authorities", "Crypto is an asset in box 3. Under the European DAC8 rules, tax authorities have exchanged data on crypto clients since 2026, including from platforms in other EU countries."], ["Crypto at a foreign platform need not be in the return, because it falls under that country's tax", "A resident of the Netherlands declares his worldwide assets, so crypto at a foreign platform as well."], ["Crypto falls in box 1, because it is regarded as income from work", "For a private investor, crypto falls in box 3 (savings and investments), not in box 1."], ["Crypto need only be in the return when Ruben sells it and takes the profit", "In box 3 the assets are taxed on the reference date, even if nothing has been sold."]], "1m.2"],
["At Vermogensadvies Peters in Utrecht, an intern asks how the anti-money-laundering rules are going to change. He has read that from 10 July 2027 the European Anti-Money Laundering Regulation (AMLR) will apply. He has also heard that a new European anti-money-laundering authority (AMLA) has been set up. He asks what this means for customer due diligence on their own clients. What is correct?", [["Customer due diligence remains; core rules move to a directly applicable regulation", "The AMLR applies directly in all EU countries, so that the rules become more uniform. Customer due diligence, reporting unusual transactions and record-keeping remain the core."], ["From 10 July 2027 AMLA itself supervises all Dutch advisory firms", "AMLA will directly supervise a limited number of large institutions; for advisory firms the national supervisor remains the point of contact."], ["The regulation applies only once the Netherlands has transposed it into its own law", "An EU regulation applies directly; only the accompanying directive (AMLD6) has to be transposed into national law."], ["For investment advice, customer due diligence lapses, because the bank already does it", "Every institution covered by the rules remains responsible for its own customer due diligence."]], "1a.7"]
];
